\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,z=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\n$$\n\nNotice that the region $R$ represents one-half of the sphere of radius $5$ centered at the origin.\n\n![](images/27/27304d8933e63e9edd2ad22fe937a9b37d05c6ae01569857e957bb1ebe5d73c0.png)\n\n \n\nAs a result, we obtain that in spherical polar coordinates, our region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ={(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)\\,:\\,0\\le \\rho \\le 5,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le \\pi ,\\,0\\le ϕ\\le \\pi }.\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the spherical polar coordinates into the function $f(x,y,z)=4\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & =f(\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ) \\\\  & =4\\sqrt{{\\rho }^{2}} \\\\  & =4\\rho \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}4\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}V & ={∭}_{Δ}4\\rho \\cdot {\\rho }^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\,dϕ\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }d\\theta \\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{5}4{\\rho }^{3}\\,d\\rho  \\\\  & =[-\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ]_{ϕ=0}^{ϕ=\\pi }\\cdot [\\theta ]_{\\theta =0}^{\\theta =\\pi }\\cdot [{\\rho }^{4}]_{\\rho =0}^{\\rho =5} \\\\  & =-(-1-1)\\cdot (\\pi -0)\\cdot ({5}^{4}-0) \\\\  & =1\\,250\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186129762 1068 #edb/ref 17592186128023 13194139533445 17592186045585 false] [17592186129762 1068 #edb/ref 17592186364149 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186129762 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186129762 1071 "The region $R$ is enclosed inside the sphere $x^{2} + y^{2} + z^{2} = 9$.\nExpress the following triple integral in spherical polar coordinates.\n$∭_(R)z\\sqrt{x^{2} + y^{2} + z^{2}}dV$" 13194139533445 17592186045585 false] [17592186129762 1071 "The region $R$ is enclosed inside the sphere ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=9.$\nExpress the following triple integral in spherical polar coordinates. \n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}z\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}V\n$$" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186129762 1074 "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,dV={∭}_{Δ}\\,f(\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ)\\,{\\rho }^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ.\n$$\n\nIf $(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)$ are the spherical coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the following formulas:\n\n$$\nx=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,y=\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,z=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\n$$\n\nNotice that the region $R$ represents the sphere of radius $3$ centered at the origin. \nAs a result, in spherical polar coordinates, our region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ={(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)\\,:\\,0\\le \\rho \\le 3,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi ,\\,0\\le ϕ\\le \\pi }.\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the spherical polar coordinates into the function $f(x,y,z)=z\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & =f(\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ) \\\\  & =\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\\cdot \\rho  \\\\  & ={\\rho }^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}z\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}V & ={∭}_{Δ}{\\rho }^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\\cdot {\\rho }^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\rho }^{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186129765 1068 #edb/ref 17592186128041 13194139533445 17592186045585 false] [17592186129765 1068 #edb/ref 17592186364155 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186129765 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186129765 1071 "![](images/41/41b5ff62b86d0cc7ce341a013f1815b7bb203f5b93cb52c525844f9bb93839b2.png)\n\nThe region $R$ is enclosed inside the sphere $x^{2} + y^{2} + z^{2} = 4y$, shown above.\nExpress the following triple integral in spherical polar coordinates.\n$∭_(R)xdV$" 13194139533445 17592186045585 false] [17592186129765 1071 "![](images/41/41b5ff62b86d0cc7ce341a013f1815b7bb203f5b93cb52c525844f9bb93839b2.png)\n\nThe region $R$ is enclosed inside the sphere ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=4y,$ shown above. \nExpress the following triple integral in spherical polar coordinates. \n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}x\\,\\text{d}V\n$$" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186129765 1074 "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,dV={∭}_{Δ}\\,f(\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ)\\,{\\rho }^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ.\n$$\n\nIf $(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)$ are the spherical coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the following formulas:\n\n$$\nx=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,y=\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,z=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\n$$\n\nNotice that the region $R$ represents the sphere of radius $2$ centered at $(0,2,0).$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now write the ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=4y$ in spherical polar coordinates:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2} & =4y \\\\ {\\rho }^{2} & =4\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ \\\\ \\rho (\\rho -4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSince $\\rho $ cannot be identical to zero on the entire sphere, we must have $\\rho =4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ.$\n\n![](images/cc/cc029278b96456462e746450c8feeb9c9ec6169a9b36f56915758ffcc1f8dd70.png)\n\n \n\nAs a result, we obtain that in spherical polar coordinates, our region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ={(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)\\,:\\,0\\le \\rho \\le 4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le \\pi ,\\,0\\le ϕ\\le \\pi }.\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the spherical polar coordinates into the function $f(x,y,z)=x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & =f(\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ) \\\\  & =\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}x\\,\\text{d}V & ={∭}_{Δ}\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\cdot {\\rho }^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ}{\\rho }^{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta {sin}^{2}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186129766 1068 #edb/ref 17592186128047 13194139533445 17592186045585 false] [17592186129766 1068 #edb/ref 17592186364161 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186129766 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186129766 1071 "The region $R$ is enclosed inside the sphere $x^{2} + y^{2} + z^{2} = 4$ for $x \\le 0$.\nExpress the following triple integral in spherical polar coordinates.\n$∭_(R)(x^{2} + y^{2})dV$" 13194139533445 17592186045585 false] [17592186129766 1071 "The region $R$ is enclosed inside the sphere ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=4$ for $x\\le 0.$\nExpress the following triple integral in spherical polar coordinates. \n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}V\n$$" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186129766 1074 "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,dV={∭}_{Δ}\\,f(\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ)\\,{\\rho }^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ.\n$$\n\nIf $(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)$ are the spherical coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the following formulas:\n\n$$\nx=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,y=\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,z=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\n$$\n\nNotice that the region $R$ represents one-half of the sphere of radius $2$ centered at the origin.\n\n![](images/de/de9f30a4f54875e05a8cedb6c73af37ad7146c56ab62d0b2b2d3f3fa359160ca.png)\n\n \n\nAs a result, we obtain that in spherical polar coordinates, our region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ={(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)\\,:\\,0\\le \\rho \\le 2,\\,\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{3\\pi }{2},\\,0\\le ϕ\\le \\pi }.\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the spherical polar coordinates into the function $f(x,y,z)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & =f(\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ) \\\\  & =(\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ)^{2}+(\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ)^{2} \\\\  & ={\\rho }^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta {sin}^{2}⁡ϕ+{\\rho }^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta {sin}^{2}⁡ϕ \\\\  & ={\\rho }^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡ϕ({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta ) \\\\  & ={\\rho }^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡ϕ\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}V & ={∭}_{Δ}{\\rho }^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡ϕ\\cdot {\\rho }^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\rho }^{4}{sin}^{3}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186129846 1068 #edb/ref 17592186128527 13194139533445 17592186045585 false] [17592186129846 1068 #edb/ref 17592186364301 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186129846 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186129846 1074 "A type II region can be written as\n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,a\\le y\\le b,\\,g(y)\\le x\\le f(y)},\n$$\n\nwhere $f$ and $g$ are continuous functions.\n\nTo express the given region as a type II region, we first rewrite all of the given curves as functions of $y:$\n\n$$\ny=2x\\,⟹\\,x=\\frac{y}{2}\n$$\n\nThe function $x=\\sqrt{y}+1$ is already written as a function of $y.$\n\nNotice that the region lies between the horizontal lines $y=1$ and $y=4.$ So, we have $1\\le y\\le 4.$\n\nWe draw a horizontal arrow through the region to determine the left function $g(y)$ and the right function $f(y).$ A horizontal line should intersect the region in a single interval only for it to be a type II region.\n\n![](images/c5/c55d5f809462b767122ccc4af97c5b4d243730ed24f655bdc18e35ec57e25ce2.png)\n\nThe horizontal line enters the region through the lower function $x=\\frac{y}{2}$ and leaves through the upper function $x=\\sqrt{y}+1.$ So, we have $\\frac{y}{2}\\le x\\le \\sqrt{y}+1.$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,1\\le y\\le 4,\\,\\frac{y}{2}\\le x\\le \\sqrt{y}+1}.\n$$" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186129847 1068 #edb/ref 17592186128533 13194139533445 17592186045585 false] [17592186129847 1068 #edb/ref 17592186364307 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186129847 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186129847 1071 "Which of the following statements are true regarding the finite region bounded between the parabola $y = 1 - x^{2}$ and the line $y =-3$?\n\n1. It is a type I region\n2. It is a type II region\n3. It's neither a type I nor a type II region" 13194139533445 17592186045585 false] [17592186129847 1071 "Which of the following statements are true regarding the finite region bounded between the parabola $y=1-{x}^{2}$ and the line $y=-3?$\n\n- It is a type I region\n- It is a type II region\n- It's neither a type I nor a type II region" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186129847 1074 "First, notice that\n\n$$\ny=1-{x}^{2}\\,⟹\\,x=\\pm \\sqrt{1-y}.\n$$\n\nNow, let's sketch our region.\n\n![](images/72/726ad8599b89029d3dba8e79ca947af99c17bd8712e551e49fc33d3e597a5cb7.png)\n\nWe have the following:\n\n- Any vertical line through the region intersects it on a single interval. Furthermore, we can represent our region as\n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,-2\\le x\\le 2,\\,-3\\le y\\le 1-{x}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nSo, this is a type I region.\n\n- Any horizontal line through the region intersects it on a single interval. Furthermore, we can represent our region as\n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,-3\\le y\\le 1,\\,-\\sqrt{1-y}\\le y\\le \\sqrt{1-y}}.\n$$\n\nSo, this is a type II region.\n\nTherefore, only statements I and II are true." 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186133814 1038 #edb/ref 17592186362777 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186133814 1038 #edb/ref 17592186362896 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186133815 1038 #edb/ref 17592186362784 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186133936 1038 #edb/ref 17592186363199 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186133936 1038 #edb/ref 17592186363206 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186133937 1038 #edb/ref 17592186363213 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186133937 1038 #edb/ref 17592186363220 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186134144 1068 #edb/ref 17592186130990 13194139533445 17592186045585 false] [17592186134144 1068 #edb/ref 17592186363193 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186134144 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186134144 1071 "![](images/d0/d0f6f5ed91981e15d2fb80e2630cec6d20f566feb627899f529e07bab12b73fb.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F = ⟨z^{2}, 2y, x^{2} + y⟩$ and the surface $S$ defined by the bounding region of a right circular cylinder of height $2$ whose base is a circle of radius $1$ centered at the origin, as shown above. Given that $S$ is positively (outward) oriented, use the divergence theorem to find the flux of $F$ through $S$." 13194139533445 17592186045585 false] [17592186134144 1071 "![](images/d0/d0f6f5ed91981e15d2fb80e2630cec6d20f566feb627899f529e07bab12b73fb.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F=⟨{z}^{2},\\,2y,\\,{x}^{2}+y⟩$ and the surface $S$ defined by the bounding region of a right circular cylinder of height $2$ whose base is a circle of radius $1$ centered at the origin, as shown above. Given that $S$ is positively (outward) oriented, use the divergence theorem to find the flux of $F$ through $S.$" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186134144 1074 "Let $R$ be a simple solid region bounded by a closed surface $S$ with positive (outward) orientation. If a vector field $F$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $R,$ then the divergence theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={∭}_{R}\\text{div}\\,F\\,\\text{d}V.\n$$\n\nComputing the divergence of $F,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{div}\\,F & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({z}^{2})+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2y)+\\frac{∂}{∂z}({x}^{2}+y) \\\\  & =0+2+0 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $R$ be the solid cylinder enclosed by $S.$ Then, using the divergence theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS & ={∭}_{R}2\\,\\text{d}V \\\\  & =2{∭}_{R}\\,\\text{d}V \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\text{Volume}(R) \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\pi \\cdot {1}^{2}\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =4\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186147493 1038 #edb/ref 17592186363944 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186147494 1038 #edb/ref 17592186363960 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186147501 1038 #edb/ref 17592186364624 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186147502 1038 #edb/ref 17592186363999 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186147512 1038 #edb/ref 17592186364006 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186147783 1068 #edb/ref 17592186144378 13194139533445 17592186045585 false] [17592186147783 1068 #edb/ref 17592186144383 13194139533445 17592186045585 false] [17592186147783 1068 #edb/ref 17592186363980 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186147783 1068 #edb/ref 17592186363985 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186147783 1068 #edb/ref 17592186363989 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186147783 1068 #edb/ref 17592186363994 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186147783 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186147783 1071 "Consider the differential equation\n$y^{″} + y = xe^{x}$.\nThe corresponding homogeneous equation has the fundamental solutions $y_{1}(x) = \\sin x$ and $y_{2}(x) = \\cos x$.\n\nSelect the correct options in the reasoning below.\n\nAccording to the method of variation of parameters, we seek a particular solution of the form\n$u_{1}\\sin x + u_{2}\\cos x$ {{field-1}}\nwhere $u_{1} = u_{1}(x), u_{2} = u_{2}(x)$, that satisfies the constraint\n$u_{1}^{′}(\\sin x)^{′} + u_{2}^{′}(\\cos x)^{′}$ {{field-2}}" 13194139533445 17592186045585 false] [17592186147783 1071 "Consider the differential equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+y=x{e}^{x}.\n$$\n\nThe corresponding homogeneous equation has the fundamental solutions ${y}_{1}(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ and ${y}_{2}(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.$ \n\nSelect the correct options in the reasoning below.\n\nAccording to the method of variation of parameters, we seek a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)={{field-1}}\n$$\n\nwhere ${u}_{1}={u}_{1}(x),{u}_{2}={u}_{2}(x),$ that satisfies the constraint\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}^{′}(x)={{field-2}}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating ${y}_{p}$ with respect to $x,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}^{′}(x)={{field-3}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following constraint equation relating ${u}_{1}^{′}$ and ${u}_{2}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n{{field-4}}\n$$" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186147783 1074 "Given a second-order linear inhomogeneous differential equation, the variation of parameters method requires that we seek a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)={u}_{1}(x){y}_{1}(x)+{u}_{2}(x){y}_{2}(x)\n$$\n\nthat satisfies the constraint\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}^{′}={u}_{1}{y}_{1}^{′}+{u}_{2}{y}_{2}^{′}.\n$$\n\nHere, ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are linearly independent solutions of the corresponding homogeneous equation, and ${u}_{1}$ and ${u}_{2}$ are functions to be determined.\n\nIn this case, we're told that the fundamental solutions are\n\n$$\n{y}_{1}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x,\\,{y}_{2}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the fundamental solutions into our expression for ${y}_{p},$ we get\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}={u}_{1}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+{u}_{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the fundamental solutions into our constraint equation for ${y}_{p}^{′},$ we get\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}^{′}={u}_{1}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)^{′}+{u}_{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)^{′}.\n$$\n\nWe can form a constraint that relates ${u}_{1}^{′}$ and ${u}_{2}^{′}$ by differentiating ${y}_{p}$ and equating this expression to our constraint on ${y}_{p}^{′}.$\n\nDifferentiating ${y}_{p}$ with respect to $x$ using the product rule, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}^{′} & ={({u}_{1}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+{u}_{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)}^{′} \\\\  & ={({u}_{1}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)}^{′}+{({u}_{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)}^{′} \\\\  & ={u}_{1}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+{u}_{1}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)^{′}+{u}_{2}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+{u}_{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)^{′} \\\\  & ={u}_{1}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+{u}_{1}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+{u}_{2}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-{u}_{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we equate our two expressions for ${y}_{p}^{′},$ and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+{u}_{1}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+{u}_{2}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-{u}_{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x & ={u}_{1}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)^{′}+{u}_{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)^{′} \\\\ {u}_{1}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+{u}_{1}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+{u}_{2}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-{u}_{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x & ={u}_{1}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-{u}_{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\ {u}_{1}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\cancel{{u}_{1}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}+{u}_{2}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\cancel{{u}_{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x} & =\\cancel{{u}_{1}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}-\\cancel{{u}_{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich finally gives\n\n$$\n{u}_{1}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+{u}_{2}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=0.\n$$" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186147790 1068 #edb/ref 17592186144444 13194139533445 17592186045585 false] [17592186147790 1068 #edb/ref 17592186364020 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186147790 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186147790 1071 "Given that for $s > ω$,\n$L{\\sinh (ωt)} = \\frac{ω}{s^{2} - ω^{2}}, L{\\cosh (ωt)} = \\frac{s}{s^{2} - ω^{2}}$,\nfind the Laplace transform of $f(t) = 2\\cosh (6t) + \\sinh (6t)$ for $s > 6$." 13194139533445 17592186045585 false] [17592186147790 1071 "Given that for $s>\\omega ,$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(\\omega t)}=\\frac{\\omega }{{s}^{2}-{\\omega }^{2}},\\,L{\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(\\omega t)}=\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}-{\\omega }^{2}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfind the Laplace transform of $f(t)=2\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(6t)+\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(6t)$ for $s>6.$" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186147790 1074 "The linearity property of the Laplace transform states that for functions $f(t)$ and $g(t),$ we have\n\n$$\nL{\\alpha f(t)+\\beta g(t)}=\\alpha \\cdot L{f(t)}+\\beta \\cdot L{g(t)},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants.\n\nTaking the Laplace transform and applying the linearity property, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{2\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(6t)+\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(6t)} & =L{2\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(6t)}+L{\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(6t)} \\\\  & =2L{\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(6t)}+L{\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(6t)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe are given that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(\\omega t)} & =\\frac{\\omega }{{s}^{2}-{\\omega }^{2}}, &  & s>\\omega , \\\\ L{\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(\\omega t)} & =\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}-{\\omega }^{2}}, &  & s>\\omega .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(6t)} & =\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}-36},\\,s>6 \\\\ L{\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(6t)} & =\\frac{6}{{s}^{2}-36},\\,s>6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Laplace transform of $f(t)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{f(t)} & =2L{\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(6t)}+L{\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(6t)} \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\frac{s}{{s}^{2}-36}+\\frac{6}{{s}^{2}-36} \\\\  & =\\frac{2s}{{s}^{2}-36}+\\frac{6}{{s}^{2}-36} \\\\  & =\\frac{2s+6}{{s}^{2}-36}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186148145 1068 #edb/ref 17592186146594 13194139533445 17592186045585 false] [17592186148145 1068 #edb/ref 17592186364187 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186148145 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186148145 1071 "Given that for $s > 0$,\n$L{\\sin ωt} = \\frac{ω}{s^{2} + ω^{2}}, L{\\cos ωt} = \\frac{s}{s^{2} + ω^{2}}$,\nfind the Laplace transform of $f(t) = 8\\cos 3t - 5\\sin 3t$ for $s > 0$." 13194139533445 17592186045585 false] [17592186148145 1071 "Given that for $s>0,$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t}=\\frac{\\omega }{{s}^{2}+{\\omega }^{2}},\\,L{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t}=\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+{\\omega }^{2}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfind the Laplace transform of  $f(t)=8\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t-5\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t$ for $s>0.$" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186148145 1074 "The linearity property of the Laplace transform states that for functions $f(t)$ and $g(t),$ we have\n\n$$\nL{\\alpha f(t)+\\beta g(t)}=\\alpha \\cdot L{f(t)}+\\beta \\cdot L{g(t)},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants.\n\nTaking the Laplace transform and applying the linearity property, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{8\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t-5\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t} & =L{8\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t}+L{-5\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t} \\\\  & =8L{\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t}-5L{\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe are given that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t} & =\\frac{\\omega }{{s}^{2}+{\\omega }^{2}}, &  & s>0, \\\\ L{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t} & =\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+{\\omega }^{2}}, &  & s>0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t} & =\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+9},\\,s>0 \\\\ L{\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t} & =\\frac{3}{{s}^{2}+9},\\,s>0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Laplace transform of $f(t)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{f(t)} & =8L{\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t}-5L{\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t} \\\\  & =8\\cdot \\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+9}-5\\cdot \\frac{3}{{s}^{2}+9} \\\\  & =\\frac{8s}{{s}^{2}+9}-\\frac{15}{{s}^{2}+9} \\\\  & =\\frac{8s-15}{{s}^{2}+9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186151806 1071 "Consider the following statement:\n\n$\\dfrac12$ is **not** a natural number, or $\\sqrt{2}$ is **not** a real number.\n\nExpress the compound statement above in symbolic form given the following:\n\n- ![](images/5a/5aa4f3afde005cff577fd75ebfa69e37861edf2628383b1191bf5c07b30fdde6.png)\n\n- ![](images/4c/4cbf587cfd90afaa031f87215c369495a23d50b886cda984b6a9a83a98ba7f06.png)" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186151806 1071 "Consider the following statement:\n\n> $\\dfrac12$ is **not** a natural number, or $\\sqrt{2}$ is **not** a real number.\n\nExpress the compound statement above in symbolic form given the following:\n\n- $\\frac{1}{2}$\n- $\\sqrt{2}$" 13194139533445 17592186045585 false] [17592186151806 1074 "The sentence\n\n $\\dfrac12$ is **not** a natural number \n\ncan be expressed symbolically as $\\neg P.$ \n\nThe sentence\n\n$\\sqrt{2}$ is **not** a real number \n\ncan be expressed symbolically as $\\neg Q.$\n\nTherefore, the sentence\n\n $\\dfrac12$ is **not** a natural number, or $\\sqrt{2}$ is **not** a real number\n\ncan be expressed symbolically as $\\neg P \\lor \\neg Q.$" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186151806 1074 "The sentence\n\n> $\\dfrac12$ is **not** a natural number\n\ncan be expressed symbolically as\n\n$$\n\\neg P\n$$\n\nThe sentence\n\n> $\\sqrt{2}$ is **not** a real number\n\ncan be expressed symbolically as\n\n$$\n\\neg Q\n$$\n\nTherefore, the sentence\n\n> $\\dfrac12$ is **not** a natural number, or $\\sqrt{2}$ is **not** a real number\n\ncan be expressed symbolically as\n\n$$\n\\neg P \\lor \\neg Q\n$$" 13194139533445 17592186045585 false] [17592186151807 1071 "Find the missing values in the following truth table:\n\n| $\\,P\\,$ | $\\,Q\\,$ | $\\, \\neg (P \\land \\neg Q)\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\phantom{F}}$ |\n| $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\phantom{F}}$ |\n| $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\phantom{F}}$ |\n| $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\phantom{F}}$ |" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186151807 1071 "Find the missing values in the following truth table:\n\n| $\\,P\\,$ | $\\,Q\\,$ | $\\, \\neg (P \\land \\neg Q)\\,$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n| $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\phantom{F}}$ |\n| $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\phantom{F}}$ |\n| $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\phantom{F}}$ |\n| $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\phantom{F}}$ |" 13194139533445 17592186045585 false] [17592186151807 1074 "First, we add