186151808 1074 "Two negations in a row cancel each other out. So: \n\n- we can simplify $\\neg(\\neg C)$ to $C,$ and\n\n- we can also simplify $\\neg(\\neg(C \\land D))$ to $C \\land D.$\n\nTherefore, the given statement can be simplified to \n\n$$\nC \\lor (C \\land D).\n$$" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186151809 1071 "Consider the following statement.\n\n$P: 1$ is **not** smaller than $2$\n\nWhat statement represents $\\neg P?$" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186151809 1071 "Consider the following statement.\n\n> $P: 1$ is **not** smaller than $2$\n\nWhich of following represents $\\neg P$?" 13194139533445 17592186045585 false] [17592186151809 1074 "The symbol \"\n\n$$\n\\neg\n$$\n\n\" represents negation. So, our task is to negate the given sentence.\n\nTo negate our plain English sentence, we place the words \"It is not true that\" in front of it. This gives the following:\n\n> It is **not** true that $1$ is **not** smaller than $2$.\n\nIn other words, $1$ is smaller than $2$. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\neg P\n$$\n\nmeans the following:\n\n> $1$ is smaller than $2$.\n\nNote that $P$ is **false,** while\n\n$$\n\\neg P\n$$\n\nis **true.**" 13194139533445 17592186045585 false] [17592186151809 1074 "The symbol \"$\\neg$\" represents negation. So, our task is to negate the given sentence. \n\nTo negate our plain English sentence, we place the words \"It is not true that\" in front of it. This gives the following:\n\nIt is **not** true that $1$ is **not** smaller than $2.$\n\nIn other words, $1$ is smaller than $2.$ Therefore, $\\neg P$ means the following:\n\n$1$ is smaller than $2.$\n\nNote that $P$ is **false,** while $\\neg P$ is **true.**" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186151823 1071 "Consider the following sets:\n\n$$\nA = \\{ 2n \\mid n \\in \\mathbb{N}\\}, \\qquad\nB = \\left\\{  2^{-n} \\mid n \\in \\mathbb {N}\\right\\}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $A = \\{ 2, 4, 6,\\ldots \\}$\n\n- $A \\cap B = \\emptyset$\n\n- $A$ and $B$ are disjoint" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186151823 1071 "Consider the following sets:\n$A = \\{2n \\mid n \\in \\mathbb{N}\\}, \\qquad B = \\left\\{2^{-n} \\mid n \\in \\mathbb {N}\\right\\}$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $A = \\{2, 4, 6,\\ldots \\}$\n2. $A \\cap B = \\emptyset$\n3. $A$ and $B$ are disjoint" 13194139533445 17592186045585 false] [17592186151823 1074 "Two sets $A$ and $B$ are *disjoint* if they have no element in common, i.e., their intersection is empty:\n\n$$\nA \\cap B = \\emptyset\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our sets.\n\n- Statement I is true.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={2n∣n\\in N} \\\\  & ={2(1),2(2),2(3),…} \\\\  & ={2,4,6,…}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. First, note that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B & ={{2}^{-n}∣n\\in N} \\\\  & ={{2}^{-1},{2}^{-2},{2}^{-3},…} \\\\  & ={\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{4},\\frac{1}{8},…}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, the intersection of $A$ and $B$ is \n\n$$\nA \\cap B = \\{ 2, 4, 6, \\ldots \\} \\cap \\left\\{\\dfrac{1}{2}, \\dfrac{1}{4}, \\dfrac{1}{8},\\ldots \\right\\} = \\emptyset.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since the intersection is empty, the two sets are disjoint.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II and III.\"" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186151823 1074 "Two sets $A$ and $B$ are *disjoint* if they have no element in common, i.e., their intersection is empty:\n\n$$\nA \\cap B = \\emptyset\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our sets.\n\n- Statement I is true.\n$A|= {2n∣n ∈ N}; = {2(1), 2(2), 2(3), …}; = {2, 4, 6, …}$\n- Statement II is true. First, note that\n$B|= {2^{-n}∣n ∈ N}; = {2^{-1}, 2^{-2}, 2^{-3}, …}; = {\\frac{1}{2}, \\frac{1}{4}, \\frac{1}{8}, …}$.\nThen, the intersection of $A$ and $B$ is\n$A \\cap B = \\{2, 4, 6, \\ldots \\} \\cap \\left\\{\\dfrac{1}{2}, \\dfrac{1}{4}, \\dfrac{1}{8},\\ldots \\right\\} = \\emptyset$.\n- Statement III is true. Since the intersection is empty, the two sets are disjoint.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II and III.\"" 13194139533445 17592186045585 false] [17592186151824 1071 "Find the disjoint union of the sets $\\{ -1, 1, \\sqrt{5} \\}$ and $\\{ v, w \\}.$" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186151824 1071 "Find the disjoint union of the sets $\\{-1, 1, \\sqrt{5} \\}$ and $\\{v, w \\}$." 13194139533445 17592186045585 false] [17592186151824 1074 "Notice that the sets are disjoint since\n\n$$\n\\{ -1, 1, \\sqrt{5} \\} \\cap \\{ v, w \\} = \\emptyset.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the disjoint union is simply the union:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{-1,1,\\sqrt{5}}⊔{v,w} & ={-1,1,\\sqrt{5}}\\cup {v,w} \\\\  & ={-1,1,\\sqrt{5},v,w}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186151824 1074 "Notice that the sets are disjoint since\n\n$$\n\\{-1, 1, \\sqrt{5} \\} \\cap \\{v, w \\} = \\emptyset\n$$\n\nTherefore, the disjoint union is simply the union:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n{-1, 1, \\sqrt{5}}⊔{v, w} &= {-1, 1, \\sqrt{5}}∪{v, w} \\\\\n&= {-1, 1, \\sqrt{5}, v, w}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533445 17592186045585 false] [17592186151825 1071 "Find the disjoint union of the sets $A_1 = \\{ 7,8 \\}$ and $A_2 = \\{ 2,4,8 \\}.$" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186151825 1071 "Find the disjoint union of the sets $A_1 = \\{7,8 \\}$ and $A_2 = \\{2,4,8 \\}$." 13194139533445 17592186045585 false] [17592186151825 1074 "Notice that the sets are overlapping since\n\n$$\n\\{ 7,8 \\} \\cap \\{ 2,4,8 \\} = \\{ 8 \\} \\neq \\emptyset.\n$$\n\nSo, for each of our sets, we form the associated set, where each element is tagged with the corresponding index, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & {A}_{1} &  & \\,\\to \\, &  & {A}_{1}^{∗}={(7,1),(8,1)} \\\\  & {A}_{2} &  & \\,\\to \\, &  & {A}_{2}^{∗}={(2,2),(4,2),(8,2)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the disjoint union of our sets is simply the union of the associated tagged sets:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1}⊔{A}_{2} & ={7,8}⊔{2,4,8} \\\\  & ={A}_{1}^{∗}\\cup {A}_{2}^{∗} \\\\  & =