re the spherical coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the following formulas:\n\n$$\nx=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,y=\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,z=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\n$$\n\nNotice that the region $R$ represents the sphere of radius $\\frac{1}{3}$ centered at the origin. \nAs a result, in spherical polar coordinates, our region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ={(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)\\,:\\,0\\le \\rho \\le \\frac{1}{3},\\,0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi ,\\,0\\le ϕ\\le \\pi }.\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the spherical polar coordinates into the function $f(x,y,z)=\\frac{yz}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & =f(\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\cdot \\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ}{{(\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ)}^{2}+{(\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{\\rho }^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ}{{\\rho }^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡ϕ({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}\\frac{yz}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}V & ={∭}_{Δ}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ}\\cdot {\\rho }^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{1/3}{\\rho }^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363027 1075 #edb/ref 17592186363028 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363027 1075