, we obtain that in spherical polar coordinates, our region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ={(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)\\,:\\,0\\le \\rho \\le \\sqrt{2},\\,\\pi \\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi ,\\,0\\le ϕ\\le \\pi }.\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the spherical polar coordinates into the function $f(x,y,z)=z\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & =f(\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ) \\\\  & =\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\\sqrt{(\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ)^{2}+(\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\\sqrt{{\\rho }^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta {sin}^{2}⁡ϕ+{\\rho }^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta {sin}^{2}⁡ϕ} \\\\  & =\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\\sqrt{{\\rho }^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡ϕ({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )} \\\\  & =\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\\sqrt{{\\rho }^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡ϕ} \\\\  & ={\\rho }^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}z\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}V & ={∭}_{Δ}{\\rho }^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\cdot {\\rho }^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{\\pi }^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}{\\rho }^{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ{sin}^{2}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363034 1075 #edb/ref 17592186363035 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363034 1075 #edb/ref 17592186363036 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363034 1075 #edb/ref 17592186363037 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363034 1075 #edb/ref 17592186363038 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363034 1075 #edb/ref 17592186363039 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363034 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139533445 17592186045585 true