{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,y=\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,z=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\n$$\n\nNotice that the region $R$ represents the sphere of radius $3$ centered at $(0,0,3).$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now write the ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=6z$ in spherical polar coordinates:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2} & =6z \\\\ {\\rho }^{2} & =6\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ \\\\ \\rho (\\rho -6\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSince $\\rho $ cannot be identical to zero on the entire sphere, we must have $\\rho =6\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ.$\n\n![](images/d1/d13b86d76d7ca5f700c75bcc9f65da43bdc4da2b013ab30ec3033d5e4b05c5c8.png)\n\n \n\nAs a result, we obtain that in spherical polar coordinates, our region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ={(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)\\,:\\,0\\le \\rho \\le 6\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi ,\\,0\\le ϕ\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}}.\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the spherical polar coordinates into the function $f(x,y,z)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & =f(\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ) \\\\  & =(\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ)^{2}+(\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ)^{2} \\\\  & ={\\rho }^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡ϕ({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta ) \\\\  & ={\\rho }^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡ϕ\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}V & ={∭}_{Δ}{\\rho }^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡ϕ\\cdot {\\rho }^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{6\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ}{\\rho }^{4}{sin}^{3}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363090 1075 #edb/ref 17592186363091 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363090 107