}+z)\\,i-z\\,j-3{x}^{2}z\\,k,$ find the flux of $F$ across $S.$" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363220 1072 #uuid "c2a8924c-323c-54ea-b830-8522b9835da1" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363220 1074 "Let $R$ be a simple solid region bounded by a closed surface $S$ with positive (outward) orientation. If a vector field $F$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $R,$ then the divergence theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={∭}_{R}\\text{div}\\,F\\,\\text{d}V.\n$$\n\nComputing the divergence of $F,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{div}\\,F & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2{x}^{3}+z)+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-z)+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(-3{x}^{2}z) \\\\  & =6{x}^{2}+0+(-3{x}^{2}) \\\\  & =3{x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $R$ be the solid region enclosed by $S.$ Then, using the divergence theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS & ={∭}_{R}3{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}V \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{1}^{3}3{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}3{x}^{2}\\text{d}x\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{3}\\text{d}y\\cdot {\\int }_{1}^{3}\\text{d}z \\\\  & =[{x}^{3}]_{x=-1}^{x=1}\\cdot (3-0)\\cdot (3-1) \\\\  & =({1}^{3}-(-1)^{3})\\cdot 3\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =12.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363221 1075 #edb/ref 17592186363222 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363221 1075 #edb/ref 17592186363223 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363221 1075 #edb/ref 17592186363224 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363221 1075 #edb/ref 17592186363225 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363221 1075 #edb/ref 17592186363226 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363221 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363221 1077 "selection" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363221 1079 #edb/ref 17592186363222 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363221 1080 #uuid "