592186363498 1074 "The equation of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nHere, the elliptical boundary of our region has horizontal radius $a=4$ and vertical radius $b=2.$ Therefore, the equation of the ellipse is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{16}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4} & =1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich we can write as\n\n$$\n4{x}^{2}+16{y}^{2}=64.\n$$\n\nWe're interested in describing all points that lie *outside* the ellipse. So, let's pick any point outside the region, say, $(5,0).$ Substituting its coordinates into the equation of the boundary, we obtain\n\n$$\n4(5)^{2}+16(0)^{2}\\,>\\,64.\n$$\n\nAs a result, the points $P(x,y)$ in our region are any real numbers that satisfy the condition\n\n$$\n4{x}^{2}+16{y}^{2}>64.\n$$\n\nWe must also include the curve $4{x}^{2}+16{y}^{2}=64$ since the boundary is included in the set (the curve is solid). So, we change the inequality symbol above as follows:\n\n$$\n4{x}^{2}+16{y}^{2}\\ge 64.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the set as\n\n$$\n{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,4{x}^{2}+16{y}^{2}\\ge 64}.\n$$" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363499 1075 #edb/ref 17592186363500 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363499 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363499 1077 "field-3" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363499 1079 #edb/ref 17592186363500 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363499 1080 #uuid "b79ae90c-0d36-5d1c-8416-c9af790b4f27" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363500 1081 "\\ge " 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363500 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363500 1084 #uuid "296014ff-439c-53ce-b7f8-4e11aadbf4a5" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363501 1075 #edb/ref 17592186363502 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [175921863