2186363562 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045500 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363562 1084 #uuid "ba0203aa-f7bb-595d-81ac-41e80db5854c" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363563 1081 "An arc of a downward-opening parabola, traversed from $(2,0)$ to $(0,0).$" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363563 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045500 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363563 1084 #uuid "5a5eef94-634a-55b7-ba52-fefb80987128" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363564 1068 #edb/ref 17592186363565 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363564 1069 "q-269893" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363564 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363564 1071 "Evaluate the **exact value** of the following geometric series:\n\n${\\sum }_{n=1}^{5}27{(\\frac{2}{3})}^{n}=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363564 1072 #uuid "21a2d84e-30a7-5626-b361-84693bdc454a" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363564 1074 "Since the given series is in the form ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{N}a{r}^{n},$ it is a geometric series. \n\nTo determine the sum, we use the formula for the sum ${S}_{N}$ of a geometric series.\n\n$$\n{S}_{N}={a}_{1}\\frac{(1-{r}^{N})}{1-r}\n$$\n\nIn this case, $r=\\frac{2}{3}$ and $N=5.$ We need to figure out the value of ${a}_{1}.$\n\nThe formula for the $n$th term of the series is\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=27{(\\frac{2}{3})}^{n},\n$$\n\nand therefore, we can calculate the first term ${a}_{1}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} & =27{(\\frac{2}{3})}^{1} \\\\  & =18\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we substitute ${a}_{1}=18,r=\\frac{2}{3}$ and $N=5$ into the formula for ${S}_{N}$, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{5}27{(\\frac{2}{3})}^{n} & =18\\cdot \\frac{(1-{(\\frac{2}{3})}^{5})}{(1-\\frac{2}{3})} \\\\  & =18\\cdot \\frac{(1-\\frac{32}{243})}{(\\frac{1}{3})} \\\\  & =18\\cdot \\frac{(\\frac{211}{243})}{(\\frac{1}{3})} \\\\  & =\\frac{422}{9}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363565 1075 #edb/ref 17592186363566 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363565 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363565 1077 "field-1" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363565 1079 #edb/ref 17592186363566 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363565 1080 #uuid "f613154c-7ed8-5313-9401-3f23528c5069" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363566 1081 "\\frac{422}{9}" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363566 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363566 1084 #uuid "3b1a664c-0362-53b6-9280-4375656408b2" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363567 1068 #edb/ref 17592186363568 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363567 1068 #edb/ref 17592186363570 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363567 1069 "q-274603" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363567 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363567 1071 "Express $2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)$ in the form $R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x+ϕ)$ with $ϕ\\in (0,{90}^{∘}).$\n\n$2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)=$\n {{field-1}}\n$\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x\\,+$\n{{field-2}}${}^{∘})$" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363567 1072 #uuid "865f3443-5472-5690-8426-199e283ca83d" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363567 1074 "The sum formula for sine gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x+ϕ) & =R(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ) \\\\  & =R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)+R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by writing the given expression in the amplitude-phase form\n\n$$\n2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x+ϕ),\n$$\n\nwe get the following identity:\n\n$$\n2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)=(R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)+(R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)$ and $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x),$ we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ=2 \\\\ R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDividing the second equation by the first, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ}{R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ} & =\\frac{2}{2} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡ϕ & =1 \\\\ ϕ & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(1) \\\\  & ={45}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find the amplitude $R.$ Squaring the first and the second equations of the system and adding the results together, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ)}^{2}+{(R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ)}^{2} & ={2}^{2}+{2}^{2} \\\\ {R}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡ϕ+{sin}^{2}⁡ϕ) & =4+4 \\\\ {R}^{2} & =8 \\\\ R & =2\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we only consider the positive root since $R>0.$\n\nFinally, we can write the initial expression in amplitude-phase form as follows:\n\n$$\n2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)=2\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x+{45}^{∘})\n$$" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363568 1075 #edb/ref 17592186363569 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363568 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363568 1077 "field-2" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363568 1079 #edb/ref 17592186363569 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363568 1080 #uuid "e01368e2-3c70-5ad0-9171-599c7b996e0b" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363569 1081 "45" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363569 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363569 1084 #uuid "2dde87e8-104d-53fe-90c9-31d1ab2a483f" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363570 1075 #edb/ref 17592186363571 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [1759218636357