ue** of the following series:\n\n${\\sum }_{n=1}^{6}\\frac{1}{3}(5)^{n-1}=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363586 1072 #uuid "54844625-659c-5def-aaa9-817595231665" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363586 1074 "Since the given series is in the form ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{N}{a}_{1}{r}^{n-1},$ it is a geometric series. \n\nTo determine the sum, we use the formula for the sum ${S}_{N}$ of a geometric series.\n\n$$\n{S}_{N}={a}_{1}\\frac{(1-{r}^{N})}{1-r}\n$$\n\nIn this case, ${a}_{1}=\\frac{1}{3},$ $r=5$ and $N=6.$ If we substitute these values into the formula for ${S}_{N}$, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{6}\\frac{1}{3}(5)^{n-1} & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot \\frac{(1-{5}^{6})}{1-5} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot \\frac{(-15\\,624)}{(-4)} \\\\  & =1\\,302\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363587 1075 #edb/ref 17592186363588 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363587 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363587 1077 "field-1" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363587 1079 #edb/ref 17592186363588 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363587 1080 #uuid "1145a294-9962-5722-898e-36a74e4868fa" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363588 1081 "1302" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363588 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363588 1084 #uuid "865637d3-a6d9-5f09-aea1-ff1b23cc2ce5" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363589 1068 #edb/ref 17592186363590 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363589 1069 "q-280122" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363589 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363589 1071 "Evaluate the **exact value** of the following series:\n\n${\\sum }_{n=1}^{5}27{(\\frac{1}{3})}^{n-1}=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363589 1072 #uuid "13b5f70c-8c89-5eb9-bf27-25f25097d8b6" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363589 1074 "Since the given series is in the form ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{N}{a}_{1}{r}^{n-1},$ it is a geometric series. \n\nTo determine the sum, we use the formula for the sum ${S}_{N}$ of a geometric series.\n\n$$\n{S}_{N}={a}_{1}\\frac{(1-{r}^{N})}{1-r}\n$$\n\nIn this case, ${a}_{1}=27,$ $r=\\frac{1}{3}$ and $N=5.$ If we substitute these values into the formula for ${S}_{N}$, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{5}27{(\\frac{1}{3})}^{n-1} & =27\\cdot \\frac{[1-{(\\frac{1}{3})}^{5}]}{(1-\\frac{1}{3})} \\\\  & =27\\cdot \\frac{[1-\\frac{1}{243}]}{(\\frac{2}{3})} \\\\  & =27\\cdot \\frac{(\\frac{242}{243})}{(\\frac{2}{3})} \\\\  & =27\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}\\cdot \\frac{242}{243} \\\\  & =\\frac{121}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363590 1075 #edb/ref 17592186363591 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363590 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363590 1077 "field-1" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363590 1079 #edb/ref 17592186363591 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363590 1080 #uuid "1d66f0c2-d21c-5381-b5f0-501ce4dede10" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363591 1081 "\\frac{121}{3}" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363591 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363591 1084 #uuid "3fc7f867-6315-53db-aea7-6dad6feb335a" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363592 1068 #edb/ref 17592186363593 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363592 1069 "q-280130" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363592 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363592 1071 "Evaluate the **exact value** of the following series:\n\n${\\sum }_{n=1}^{6}\\frac{6}{7}{(\\frac{1}{3})}^{n-1}=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363592 1072 #uuid "7d1fabc2-e7bb-5b2a-aec1-df3a275e51e5" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363592 1074 "Since the given series is in the form ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{N}{a}_{1}{r}^{n-1},$ it is a geometric series. \n\nTo determine the sum, we use the formula for the sum ${S}_{N}$ of a geometric series.\n\n$$\n{S}_{N}={a}_{1}\\frac{(1-{r}^{N})}{1-r}\n$$\n\nIn this case, ${a}_{1}=\\frac{6}{7},$ $r=\\frac{1}{3}$ and $N=6.$ If we substitute these values into the formula for ${S}_{N}$, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{6}\\frac{6}{7}{(\\frac{1}{3})}^{n-1} & =\\frac{6}{7}\\cdot \\frac{[1-{(\\frac{1}{3})}^{6}]}{(1-\\frac{1}{3})} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{7}\\cdot \\frac{[1-\\frac{1}{729}]}{(\\frac{2}{3})} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{7}\\cdot \\frac{(\\frac{728}{729})}{(\\frac{2}{3})} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{7}\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}\\cdot \\frac{728}{729} \\\\  & =\\frac{18}{14}\\cdot \\frac{728}{729} \\\\  & =\\frac{104}{81}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363593 1075 #edb/ref 17592186363594 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363593 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363593 1077 "field-1" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363593 1079 #edb/ref 17592186363594 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363593 1080 #uuid "648da91f-b980-5e22-a9cf-75d8944e3678" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363594 1081 "\\frac{104}{81}" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363594 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363594 1084 #uuid "bc63cab3-7153-5953-abb8-3d274837673d" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363595 1068 #edb/ref 17592186363596 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363595 1069 "q-280241" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363595 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363595 1071 "Evaluate the **exact value** of the following geometric series:\n\n${\\sum }_{n=1}^{4}\\frac{20}{{5}^{n}}=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363595 1072 #uuid "1bb9f00b-8103-5e1f-83ed-4609bebc9146" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363595 1074 "Notice that we can rewrite the series as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{4}\\frac{20}{{5}^{n}}={\\sum }_{n=1}^{4}20\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{5})}^{n}\n$$\n\nSince the above series is in the form ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{N}a{r}^{n},$ it is a geometric series. \n\nTo determine the sum, we use the formula for the sum ${S}_{N}$ of a geometric series.\n\n$$\n{S}_{N}={a}_{1}\\frac{(1-{r}^{N})}{1-r}\n$$\n\nIn this case, $r=\\frac{1}{5}$ and $N=4.$ We need to figure out ${a}_{1}.$\n\nThe formula for the $n$th term of the series is\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=20\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{5})}^{n},\n$$\n\nand therefore the first term of the series is\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=20\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{5})}^{1}=4.\n$$\n\nIf we substitute ${a}_{1}=4$,  $r=\\frac{1}{5}$ and $N=4$ into the formula for ${S}_{N}$, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{4}20\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{5})}^{n} & =4\\cdot \\frac{[1-{(\\frac{1}{5})}^{4}]}{(1-\\frac{1}{5})} \\\\  & =4\\cdot \\frac{(1-\\frac{1}{625})}{\\frac{4}{5}} \\\\  & =4\\cdot \\frac{5}{4}\\cdot \\frac{624}{625} \\\\  & =\\frac{624}{125}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363596 1075 #edb/ref 17592186363597 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363596 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363596 1077 "field-1" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363596 1079 #edb/ref 17592186363597 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363596 1080 #uuid "3d4229e1-9725-5cb2-9465-30128ff2d523" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363597 1081 "\\frac{624}{125}" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363597 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363597 1084 #uuid "266a2c45-5bfd-518d-a709-2297ec872f83" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363598 1068 #edb/ref 17592186363599 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363598 1069 "q-280242" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363598 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363598 1071 "Evaluate the **exact value** of the following geometric series:\n\n${\\sum }_{n=1}^{4}\\frac{10}{{4}^{n}}=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363598 1072 #uuid "05821a4d-c501-591c-92bc-bed9013bc02b" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363598 1074 "Notice that we can rewrite the series as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{4}\\frac{10}{{4}^{n}}={\\sum }_{n=1}^{4}10\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{4})}^{n}\n$$\n\nSince the above series is in the form ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{N}a{r}^{n},$ it is a geometric series. \n\nTo determine the sum, we use the formula for the sum ${S}_{N}$ of a geometric series.\n\n$$\n{S}_{N}={a}_{1}\\frac{(1-{r}^{N})}{1-r}\n$$\n\nIn this case, $r=\\frac{1}{4}$ and $N=4.$ We need to figure out ${a}_{1}.$\n\nThe formula for the $n$th term of the series is\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=10\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{4})}^{n},\n$$\n\nand therefore the first term of the series is\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=10\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{4})}^{1}=\\frac{5}{2}.\n$$\n\nIf we substitute ${a}_{1}=\\frac{5}{2},r=\\frac{1}{4}$ and $N=4$ into the formula for ${S}_{N}$, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{4}10\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{4})}^{n} & =\\frac{5}{2}\\cdot \\frac{[1-{(\\frac{1}{4})}^{4}]}{(1-\\frac{1}{4})} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2}\\cdot \\frac{(1-\\frac{1}{256})}{\\frac{3}{4}} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2}\\cdot \\frac{4}{3}\\cdot \\frac{255}{256} \\\\  & =\\frac{425}{128}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363599 1075 #edb/ref 17592186363600 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363599 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363599 1077 "field-1" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363599 1079 #edb/ref 17592186363600 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363599 1080 #uuid "69b631f0-4056-5b0e-9a52-77aefe2f5251" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363600 1081 "\\frac{425}{128}" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363600 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363600 1084 #uuid "cd91bb7a-2cd9-565f-bb46-887e3287819c" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363601 1068 #edb/ref 17592186363602 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363601 1069 "q-280304" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363601 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363601 1071 "Determine the value of the following series rounded to $3$ **decimal places.**\n\n${\\sum }_{n=0}^{6}7{(\\frac{1}{4})}^{n-1}≈$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363601 1072 #uuid "a6392e25-1915-5425-b6f4-a97150ebd142" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363601 1074 "Since the given series is in the form $\\sum a{r}^{n-1},$ it is a geometric series. Notice that the index starts from $n=0$ instead of $n=1.$\n\nTo determine the sum, we use the formula for the sum ${S}_{N}$ of a geometric series.\n\n$$\n{S}_{N}={a}_{0}\\frac{(1-{r}^{N})}{1-r}\n$$\n\nIn this case, $r=\\frac{1}{4}.$ Also, note that $N=7,$ since there are $7$ terms in total.\n\nThe formula for the $n$th term of the series is\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=7{(\\frac{1}{4})}^{n-1},\n$$\n\nand so the first term ${a}_{0}$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{0} & =7{(\\frac{1}{4})}^{-1} \\\\  & =7(\\frac{4}{1}) \\\\  & =28.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we substitute ${a}_{0}=28,r=\\frac{1}{4},N=7$ into the formula for ${S}_{N}$, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{7} & =28\\cdot \\frac{[1-{(\\frac{1}{4})}^{7}]}{(1-\\frac{1}{4})} \\\\  & =28\\cdot \\frac{[1-{(\\frac{1}{4})}^{7}]}{(\\frac{3}{4})} \\\\  & =\\frac{112}{3}\\cdot [1-{(\\frac{1}{4})}^{7}] \\\\  & ≈37.331\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that the final answer has been rounded to three decimal places." 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363602 1075 #edb/ref 17592186363603 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363602 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363602 1077 "field-1" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363602 1079 #edb/ref 17592186363603 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363602 1080 #uuid "42f0c0c0-ef4d-5133-9495-738560fcc24f" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363603 1081 "37.331" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363603 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363603 1084 #uuid "6513ab93-14d9-51a0-80d4-2aae2cae9735" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363604 1068 #edb/ref 17592186363605 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363604 1069 "q-280306" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363604 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363604 1071 "Determine the value of the following series rounded to $3$ **decimal places.**\n\n${\\sum }_{n=0}^{5}5{(\\frac{1}{3})}^{n-1}≈$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363604 1072 #uuid "7124dfa8-23c2-507e-b39b-d2061493f76f" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363604 1074 "Since the given series is in the form $\\sum a{r}^{n-1},$ it is a geometric series. Notice that the index starts from $n=0$ instead of $n=1.$\n\nTo determine the sum, we use the formula for the sum ${S}_{N}$ of a geometric series.\n\n$$\n{S}_{N}={a}_{0}\\frac{(1-{r}^{N})}{1-r}\n$$\n\nIn this case, $r=\\frac{1}{3}.$ Also, note that $N=6,$ since there are $6$ terms in total.\n\nThe formula for the $n$th term of the series is\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=5{(\\frac{1}{3})}^{n-1},\n$$\n\nand so the first term ${a}_{0}$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{0} & =5{(\\frac{1}{3})}^{-1} \\\\  & =5(\\frac{3}{1}) \\\\  & =15.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we substitute ${a}_{0}=15,r=\\frac{1}{3},N=6$ into the formula for ${S}_{N}$, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{6} & =15\\cdot \\frac{[1-{(\\frac{1}{3})}^{6}]}{(1-\\frac{1}{3})} \\\\  & =15\\cdot \\frac{[1-{(\\frac{1}{3})}^{6}]}{(\\frac{2}{3})} \\\\  & =\\frac{45}{2}\\cdot [1-{(\\frac{1}{3})}^{6}] \\\\  & ≈22.469\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that the final answer has been rounded to three decimal places." 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363605 1075 #edb/ref 17592186363606 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363605 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363605 1077 "field-1" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363605 1079 #edb/ref 17592186363606 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363605 1080 #uuid "339007e1-75d5-5f6f-b071-c808c79cb141" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363606 1081 "22.469" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363606 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363606 1084 #uuid "de7d6b46-1ff6-5663-a618-09a066b39544" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363607 1068 #edb/ref 17592186363608 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363607 1069 "q-30023" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363607 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363607 1071 "![](images/d5/d5beca679a437f9842791bc6125d94c86e095790ea2e8cd8663316e7a518072a.png)\n\nAn elastic rectangular prism whose height is $20\\text{ in}$ and width is $30\\text{ in}$ is being stretched. If its length is increasing at a rate of $10\\text{ in/min}$, what is the rate of change of the surface area of the rectangular prism?" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363607 1072 #uuid "ff5a85ff-0929-5603-927c-2bcbc45ac290" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363607 1074 "Let $x,y,z$ be the dimensions of the rectangular prism, let $S$ be its surface area, and let $V$ be its volume. The variables $x,S$ and $V$ change with time. In particular, we are given that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =20\\text{ in}, \\\\ z & =30\\text{ in}, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =10\\text{ in}\\text{/min}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe would like to find $\\frac{\\text{d}S}{\\text{d}t}.$\n\nWe want to find the rate of change of $S$ knowing the rate of change of $x.$ So, we start with the formula for the surface area of the rectangular prism:\n\n$$\nS=2xz+2xy+2yz\n$$\n\nSubstituting the known values, we get\n\n$$\nS=2(30)(x)+2x(20)+2(30)(20)=100x+1\\,200.\n$$\n\nNow, we differentiate with respect to time using the chain rule, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}S}{\\text{d}t} & =100(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the above expression using $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=10,$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}S}{\\text{d}t} & =100(10)=1\\,000{\\text{ in}}^{2}\\text{/min}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the rectangular prism's surface area is increasing at a rate of $1\\,000\\,{\\text{in}}^{2}/\\text{min}.$" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363608 1075 #edb/ref 17592186363609 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363608 1075 #edb/ref 17592186363610 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363608 1075 #edb/ref 17592186363611 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363608 1075 #edb/ref 17592186363612 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363608 1075 #edb/ref 17592186363613 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363608 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363608 1077 "selection" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363608 1079 #edb/ref 17592186363612 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363608 1080 #uuid "8e4b6511-b251-5894-9d2f-4b2250b4cf4f" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363609 1081 "1\\,460{\\text{ in}}^{2}\\text{/min}" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363609 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363609 1084 #uuid "57736546-eef3-5116-9064-531d0425d0ec" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363610 1081 "1\\,170{\\text{ in}}^{2}\\text{/min}" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363610 1083 #edb/ref 1