atorname{cos}⁡(6x-ϕ) & =R(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ) \\\\  & =R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6x)+R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by writing the given expression in the amplitude-phase form\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6x)+4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6x)=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6x-ϕ),\n$$\n\nwe get the following identity:\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6x)+4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6x)=(R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6x)+(R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6x).\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6x)$ and $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6x),$ we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ=4 \\\\ R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ=\\sqrt{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDividing the second equation by the first, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ}{R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ} & =\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{4} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡ϕ & =\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{4} \\\\ ϕ & =arctan\\,(\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{4})≈0.510\\text{ }\\text{rad},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places.\n\nNow, we find the amplitude $R.$ Squaring the first and the second equations of the system and adding the results together, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ)^{2}+(R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ)^{2} & ={4}^{2}+(\\sqrt{5})^{2} \\\\ {R}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡ϕ+{sin}^{2}⁡ϕ) & =16+5 \\\\ {R}^{2} & =21 \\\\ R & =\\sqrt{21}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we only consider the positive root since $R>0.$\n\nFinally, we can write the initial expression in amplitude-phase form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6x)+4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6x)≈\\sqrt{21}\\,cos\\,(6x-0.510).\n$$" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363685 1075 #edb/ref 17592186363686 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363685 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363685 1