(18\\,\\frac{\\text{L}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =(0.14\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\cancel{\\text{L}}})\\cdot (18\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =2.52\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Calculating the rate at which dissolved chemical exits the tank.\n\nNow, to find the rate at which dissolved chemical leaves the tank, we must first determine the volume of solution in the tank at time $t.$\n\n- The tank initially contains $320\\,\\text{L}$ of solution.\n\n- Chemical solution enters the tank at a rate of $18\\,\\text{L/min}.$ So, after $t$ minutes, $18t\\,\\text{L}$ have entered the tank.\n\n- The completely mixed solution leaves the tank at a rate of $18\\,\\text{L/min}.$ So, after $t$ minutes, $18t\\,\\text{L}$ have left the tank.\n\nAs a result, the volume of solution in the tank after $t$ minutes is \n\n$$\n320+18t-18t=320\\,\\text{L}.\n$$\n\nSince $Q$ is the mass of dissolved chemical in the tank, the *concentration* of the solution at time $t$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{Q}{320}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{L}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, as the completely mixed solution leaves at a rate of $18\\,\\text{L/min},$ *dissolved chemical* leaves at a rate of\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} & =(18\\,\\frac{\\text{L}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{320}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{L}}) \\\\  & =(18\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{320}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{18Q}{320}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}} \\\\  & =\\frac{9Q}{160}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Calculate the overall rate of change.\n\nFinally, the rate of change equals the rate of entry minus the rate of exit: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt} & ={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} \\\\  & =2.52-\\frac{9Q}{160}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich finally gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{9}{160}Q=2.52.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533445 17592186045585 