9533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363874 1074 "Let $Q$ be the amount of reactant in the reactor at time $t.$ Then, the derivative \n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}\n$$\n\ndescribes how this amount changes with respect to time $t,$ in minutes. This is equal to the rate at which reactant enters the reactor minus the rate at which reactant leaves the reactor.\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}\n$$\n\nNote that the units of $\\frac{dQ}{dt},{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}$ and ${Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}$ are $\\text{lb}/\\text{min}.$ Now, let's compute the required rates of change in steps.\n\n**Step 1**: Calculating the rate at which reactant enters the reactor.\n\nFirst, note that pure solvent (i.e., a solution containing $0\\,\\text{lb/gal}$ of reactant) enters the reactor at a rate of $6\\,\\text{gal/min}.$ So, the rate at which the *reactant* enters the reactor is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′} & =(0\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{gal}})\\cdot (6\\,\\frac{\\text{gal}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =(0\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}})\\cdot (6\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =0\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Calculating the rate at which reactant exits the reactor.\n\nTo find the rate at which reactant leaves the reactor, we must first determine the volume of solution in the reactor at time $t.$\n\nThe reactor initially contains $190\\,\\text{gal}$ of solution; pure solvent enters at $6\\,\\text{gal/min},$ and mixed solution leaves at $4\\,\\text{gal/min}.$\nAs a result, the amount of solution in the reactor after $t$ minutes is \n\n$$\nV(t)=190+6t-4t=2(95+t)\\,\\text{gal}.\n$$\n\nNote that $V(t)$ is an increasing function, so the reactor is filling.\n\nSince $Q$ is the amount of reactant in the reactor, the concentration of the solution at time $t$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{Q}{2(95+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{gal}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, as the completely mixed solution leaves at a rate of $4\\,\\text{gal/min},$ *reactant* leaves at a rate of\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} & =(4\\,\\frac{\\text{gal}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{2(95+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{gal}}) \\\\  & =(4\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{2(95+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2Q}{95+t}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Calculate the overall rate of change.\n\nThe rate of change equals the rate of entry minus the rate of exit. Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt} & ={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} \\\\  & =0-\\frac{2Q}{95+t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}=-\\frac{2}{95+t}\\,Q.\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Integrate the equation\n\nSeparating variables and integrating both sides with respect to $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{Q}\\cdot \\frac{dQ}{dt} & =-\\frac{4}{190+2t} \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{Q}\\cdot \\frac{dQ}{dt}dt & =\\int (-\\frac{4}{190+2t})\\,dt \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{Q}\\cdot \\,dQ & =-2\\int \\frac{1}{95+t}\\,dt \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|Q| & =-2(\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|95+t|)+C \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|Q| & =-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|95+t|+C \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|Q| & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|(95+t)^{-2}|+C \\\\ |Q| & ={e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡|(95+t)^{-2}|+C} \\\\ Q(t) & =\\frac{A}{(95+t)^{2}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $A=\\pm {e}^{C}$ is a constant of integration.\n\nWe're told that the reactor initially contains $32\\,\\text{lb}$ of reactant. So, we can apply the initial condition $Q(0)=32,$ and solve for $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(0) & =32 \\\\ \\frac{A}{(95+0)^{2}} & =32 \\\\ A & =32(95)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain the following expression for $Q(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(t) & =\\frac{32(190)^{2}}{(95+t)^{2}} \\\\  & =32{(\\frac{95}{95+t})}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let ${t}_{o}$ be the time when the reactor begins to overflow. The reactor has a capacity of $310\\,\\text{gal},$ so we can use the condition $V({t}_{o})=310$ to solve for ${t}_{o}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V({t}_{o}) & =310 \\\\ 190+2{t}_{o} & =310 \\\\ 2{t}_{o} & =120 \\\\ {t}_{o} & =60\\,\\text{min}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the amount of reactant in the reactor at the moment it begins to overflow is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(60) & =32{(\\frac{95}{95+60})}^{2} \\\\  & =32{(\\frac{95}{155})}^{2} \\\\  & ≈12.02\\,\\text{lb},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to two decimal places." 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363875 1075 #edb/ref 17592186363876 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363875 1075 #edb/ref 17592186363877 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363875 1075 #edb/ref 17592186363878 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363875 1075 #edb/ref 17592186363879 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363875 1075 #edb/ref 17592186363880 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363875 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363875 1077 "selection" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363875 1079 #edb/ref 17592186363880 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363875 1080 #uuid "617c7a09-689e-563c-b4f9-daf6d3871ab7" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363876 1081 "11.60\\,\\text{lb}" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363876 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363876 1084 #uuid "7cb4e1cb-a4d9-5a79-8bad-83176cb4a640" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363877 1081 "11.85\\,\\text{lb}" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363877 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363877 1084 #uuid "97b47761-bf18-5c2c-807b-5fbd6eecd397" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363878 1081 "12.46\\,\\text{lb}" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363878 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363878 1084 #uuid "f9316b8e-3e20-5a4c-8d57-cf7e0b5d688e" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363879 1081 "12.19\\,\\text{lb}" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363879 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363879 1084 #uuid "8dde20d6-6676-5b10-94da-9eee8eb3183f" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363880 1081 "12.02\\,\\text{lb}" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363880 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363880 1084 #uuid "07f0a2a1-f819-5785-94c7-449359e5a659" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363881 1068 #edb/ref 17592186363882 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363881 1069 "q-336826" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363881 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363881 1071 "A $9\\,500\\,\\text{mL}$-capacity vat initially holds $6\\,500\\,\\text{mL}$ of a sugar-water solution containing $300\\,\\text{g}$ of sugar. Pure water begins to flow into the vat at a rate of $100\\,\\text{mL/s},$ while the completely mixed sugar-water flows out at a rate of $80\\,\\text{mL/s}.$ Find, rounded to two decimal places, the amount of sugar in the vat at the moment it begins to overflow." 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363881 1072 #uuid "98bdf48f-bd8c-581b-9efb-c84bc03b4bb3" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363881 1074 "Let $Q$ be the amount of sugar in the vat at time $t.$ Then, the derivative \n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}\n$$\n\ndescribes how this amount changes with respect to time $t,$ in seconds. This is equal to the rate at which sugar enters the vat minus the rate at which sugar leaves the vat.\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}\n$$\n\nNote that the units of $\\frac{dQ}{dt},{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}$ and ${Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}$ are $\\text{g}/\\text{s}.$ Now, let's compute the required rates of change in steps.\n\n**Step 1**: Calculating the rate at which sugar enters the vat.\n\nFirst, note that pure water (i.e., a solution containing $0\\,\\text{g/mL}$ of sugar) enters the vat at a rate of $100\\,\\text{mL/s}.$ So, the rate at which the *sugar* enters the vat is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′} & =(0\\,\\frac{\\text{g}}{\\text{mL}})\\cdot (100\\,\\frac{\\text{mL}}{\\text{s}}) \\\\  & =(0\\,\\frac{\\text{g}}{\\cancel{\\text{mL}}})\\cdot (100\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{mL}}}{\\text{s}}) \\\\  & =0\\,\\frac{\\text{g}}{\\text{s}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Calculating the rate at which sugar exits the vat.\n\nTo find the rate at which sugar leaves the vat, we must first determine the volume of sugar-water in the vat at time $t.$\n\nThe vat initially contains $6\\,500\\,\\text{mL}$ of solution; pure water enters at $100\\,\\text{mL/s},$ and mixed solution leaves at $80\\,\\text{mL/s}.$\nAs a result, the amount of solution in the vat after $t$ seconds is \n\n$$\nV(t)=6\\,500+100t-80t=20(325+t)\\,\\text{mL}.\n$$\n\nNote that $V(t)$ is an increasing function, so the vat is filling.\n\nSince $Q$ is the amount of sugar in the vat, the concentration of the solution at time $t$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{Q}{20(325+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{g}}{\\text{mL}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, as the completely mixed solution leaves at a rate of $80\\,\\text{mL/s},$ *sugar* leaves at a rate of\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} & =(80\\,\\frac{\\text{mL}}{\\text{s}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{20(325+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{g}}{\\text{mL}}) \\\\  & =(80\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{mL}}}{\\text{s}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{20(325+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{g}}{\\cancel{\\text{mL}}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{4Q}{325+t}\\,\\frac{\\text{g}}{\\text{s}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Calculate the overall rate of change.\n\nThe rate of change equals the rate of entry minus the rate of exit. Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt} & ={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} \\\\  & =0-\\frac{4Q}{325+t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}=-\\frac{4}{325+t}\\,Q.\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Integrate the equation\n\nSeparating variables and integrating both sides with respect to $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{Q}\\cdot \\frac{dQ}{dt} & =-\\frac{4}{325+t} \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{Q}\\cdot \\frac{dQ}{dt}dt & =\\int (-\\frac{4}{325+t})\\,dt \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{Q}\\cdot \\,dQ & =-4\\int \\frac{1}{325+t}\\,dt \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|Q| & =-4(\\operatorname{ln}⁡|325+t|)+C \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|Q| & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|(325+t)^{-4}|+C \\\\ |Q| & ={e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡|(325+t)^{-4}|+C} \\\\ Q(t) & =\\frac{A}{(325+t)^{4}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $A=\\pm {e}^{C}$ is a constant of integration.\n\nWe're told that the vat initially contains $300\\,\\text{g}$ of sugar. So, we can apply the initial condition $Q(0)=300,$ and solve for $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(0) & =300 \\\\ \\frac{A}{(325+0)^{4}} & =300 \\\\ A & =300(325)^{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain the following expression for $Q(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(t) & =\\frac{300(325)^{4}}{(325+t)^{4}} \\\\  & =300{(\\frac{325}{325+t})}^{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let ${t}_{o}$ be the time when the vat begins to overflow. The vat has a capacity of $9\\,500\\,\\text{mL},$ so we can use the condition $V({t}_{o})=9\\,500$ to solve for ${t}_{o}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V({t}_{o}) & =9\\,500 \\\\ 20(325+{t}_{o}) & =9\\,500 \\\\ 325+{t}_{o} & =475 \\\\ {t}_{o} & =150\\,\\text{s}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the amount of sugar in the vat at the moment it begins to overflow is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(150) & =300{(\\frac{325}{325+150})}^{4} \\\\  & =300{(\\frac{13}{19})}^{4} \\\\  & ≈65.75\\,\\text{g},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to two decimal places." 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363882 1075 #edb/ref 17592186363883 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363882 1075 #edb/ref 17592186363884 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363882 1075 #edb/ref 17592186363885 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363882 1075 #edb/ref 17592186363886 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363882 1075 #edb/ref 17592186363887 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363882 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363882 1077 "selection" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363882 1079 #edb/ref 17592186363884 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363882 1080 #uuid "8cfba8ca-3fd1-51fd-9b05-5695d3defec3" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363883 1081 "67.49\\,\\text{g}" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363883 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363883 1084 #uuid "89eecff6-8175-550d-ad43-2b9766bd7fa6" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363884 1081 "65.75\\,\\text{g}" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363884 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363884 1084 #uuid "0ed6a48d-1428-52d9-a522-2bee31663016" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363885 1081 "63.98\\,\\text{g}" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363885 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363885 1084 #uuid "17ca700c-b4a3-5002-abdc-7f9a8e703bc5" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363886 1081 "70.10\\,\\text{g}" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363886 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363886 1084 #uuid "51f09e87-40c3-5d6f-9b67-9acbd58b27a8" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363887 1081 "61.42\\,\\text{g}" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363887 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363887 1084 #uuid "5118b033-cfac-501e-bbca-b3f1d3ca44b1" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363888 1068 #edb/ref 17592186363889 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363888 1069 "q-337309" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363888 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363888 1071 "Determine the differential equation satisfied by the orthogonal trajectories of the family of curves ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=-3C,$ where $C\\ne 0$ is a parameter." 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363888 1072 #uuid "c92ecfc3-f93a-50d9-90e9-67f1f523a0e0" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363888 1074 "Orthogonal trajectories are families of curves that intersect another given family of curves at right angles. In other words, the tangent lines of curves from each family are perpendicular at every intersection point.\n\nLet's determine the slope of the family of curves given by ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=-3C.$ First, we implicitly differentiate with respect to $x$ and solve for the derivative of $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{d}{dx}\\,({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}) & =\\frac{d}{dx}\\,(-3C) \\\\ 2x+2y{y}^{′} & =0 \\\\ 2y{y}^{′} & =-2x \\\\ {y}^{′} & =-\\frac{x}{y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that the parameter $C$ has already been eliminated.\n\nFor a curve to intersect this family orthogonally, its slope ${y}_{⊥}^{′}$ must be the negative reciprocal of the slope ${y}^{′}$ above. Hence,\n\n$$\n{y}_{⊥}^{′}=-\\frac{1}{{y}^{′}}=-\\frac{1}{(-\\frac{x}{y})}=\\frac{y}{x}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the orthogonal trajectories must satisfy\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{y}{x}.\n$$\n\n**Note**: Since $C\\ne 0,$ the given family has $x\\ne 0$ and $y\\ne 0,$ so both ${y}^{′}$ and ${y}_{⊥}^{′}$ are well-defined." 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363889 1075 #edb/ref 17592186363890 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363889 1075 #edb/ref 17592186363891 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363889 1075 #edb/ref 17592186363892 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363889 1075 #edb/ref 17592186363893 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363889 1075 #edb/ref 17592186363894 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363889 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363889 1077 "selection" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363889 1079 #edb/ref 17592186363894 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363889 1080 #uuid "1453cd82-44b1-5060-9500-9243905809f4" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363890 1081 "\\frac{dy}{dx}=-\\frac{x}{y}" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363890 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363890 1084 #uuid "892db606-dc68-5a2b-87e0-a28731ddb6cf" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363891 1081 "\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{x}{y}" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363891 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363891 1084 #uuid "42aaa8c8-f2a6-522d-8b1c-e8426a3e5508" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363892 1081 "\\frac{dy}{dx}=-\\frac{y}{x}" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363892 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363892 1084 #uuid "43830384-21b6-5804-9e1a-56c908a1bd06" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363893 1081 "\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}}" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363893 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363893 1084 #uuid "b2035ed7-ba64-520f-9730-bc1fa05c95d3" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363894 1081 "\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{y}{x}" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363894 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363894 1084 #uuid "686b55d7-ff12-5c61-9938-3d2f3657f2b0" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363895 1068 #edb/ref 17592186363896 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363895 1069 "q-337318" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363895 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363895 1071 "Determine the differential equation satisfied by the orthogonal trajectories of the family of curves ${y}^{2}=3C{x}^{2},$ where $C\\ne 0$ is a parameter, in terms of $x$ and $y$ only." 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363895 1072 #uuid "8ee2050b-6343-5391-af1d-62fa3677d5ac" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363895 1074 "Orthogonal trajectories are families of curves that intersect another given family of curves at right angles. In other words, the tangent lines of curves from each family are perpendicular at every intersection point.\n\nLet's determine the slope of the family of curves given by ${y}^{2}=3C{x}^{2}.$ First, we implicitly differentiate with respect to $x$ and solve for the derivative of $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{d}{dx}({y}^{2}) & =\\frac{d}{dx}(3C{x}^{2}) \\\\ 2y{y}^{′} & =6Cx \\\\ {y}^{′} & =\\frac{3Cx}{y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we must eliminate $C.$ From the original family of curves equation, we have\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}=3C{x}^{2}\\,⟹\\,C=\\frac{{y}^{2}}{3{x}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into our slope equation, we get\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\frac{3(\\frac{{y}^{2}}{3{x}^{2}})x}{y}=\\frac{y}{x}.\n$$\n\nFor a curve to intersect this family orthogonally, its slope ${y}_{⊥}^{′}$ must be the negative reciprocal of the slope ${y}^{′}$ above. Hence,\n\n$$\n{y}_{⊥}^{′}=-\\frac{1}{{y}^{′}}=-\\frac{1}{(\\frac{y}{x})}=-\\frac{x}{y}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the orthogonal trajectories must satisfy\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=-\\frac{x}{y}.\n$$\n\n**Note**: For the original family, since $C\\ne 0,$ we have $y\\ne 0$ whenever $x\\ne 0,$ hence, the slopes are well-defined. Otherwise, when $x=0,$ the curve forces $y=0,$ and the tangent slope is not defined." 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363896 1075 #edb/ref 17592186363897 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363896 1075 #edb/ref 17592186363898 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363896 1075 #edb/ref 17592186363899 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363896 1075 #edb/ref 17592186363900 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363896 1075 #edb/ref 17592186363901 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363896 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363896 1077 "selection" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363896 1079 #edb/ref 17592186363897 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363896 1080 #uuid "b771a122-3d76-5ebf-816a-c5858ca422d5" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363897 1081 "\\frac{dy}{dx}=-\\frac{x}{y}" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363897 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363897 1084 #uuid "1b7d7c47-ebe9-526c-af96-6ccd2a09c50d" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363898 1081 "\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{x}{y}" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363898 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363898 1084 #uuid "b470dd12-2a32-584e-8258-91a256b16ef1" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363899 1081 "\\frac{dy}{dx}=-\\frac{y}{x}" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363899 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363899 1084 #uuid "44ee728c-a095-50db-ada8-69908704d150" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363900 1081 "\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{{y}^{2}}{x}" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363900 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363900 1084 #uuid "8370c006-2559-5d7b-9836-b25487e91c21" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363901 1081 "\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{y}{x}" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363901 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363901 1084 #uuid "5a8493b7-db04-5790-b2e7-6e3289b58a32" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363902 1068 #edb/ref 17592186363903 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363902 1069 "q-337322" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363902 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363902 1071 "Determine the differential equation satisfied by the orthogonal trajectories of the family of curves ${y}^{2}=-\\frac{4{C}^{2}}{x},$ where $C\\ne 0$ is a parameter, in terms of $x$ and $y$ only." 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363902 1072 #uuid "6d9a7bf7-c360-5b6d-8323-d7ffd06fced2" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363902 1074 "Orthogonal trajectories are families of curves that intersect another given family of curves at right angles. In other words, the tangent lines of curves from each family are perpendicular at every intersection point.\n\nLet's determine the slope of the family of curves given by ${y}^{2}=-\\frac{4{C}^{2}}{x}.$ First, we implicitly differentiate with respect to $x$ and solve for the derivative of $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{d}{dx}({y}^{2}) & =\\frac{d}{dx}\\,(-\\frac{4{C}^{2}}{x}) \\\\ 2y{y}^{′} & =\\frac{4{C}^{2}}{{x}^{2}} \\\\ {y}^{′} & =\\frac{2{C}^{2}}{{x}^{2}y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we must eliminate $C.$ From the original family of curves equation, we have\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}=-\\frac{4{C}^{2}}{x}\\,⟹\\,{C}^{2}=-\\frac{x{y}^{2}}{4}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into our slope equation, we get\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\frac{2(-\\frac{x{y}^{2}}{4})}{{x}^{2}y}=-\\frac{y}{2x}.\n$$\n\nFor a curve to intersect this family orthogonally, its slope ${y}_{⊥}^{′}$ must be the negative reciprocal of the slope ${y}^{′}$ above. Hence,\n\n$$\n{y}_{⊥}^{′}=-\\frac{1}{{y}^{′}}=-\\frac{1}{(-\\frac{y}{2x})}=\\frac{2x}{y}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the orthogonal trajectories must satisfy\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{2x}{y}.\n$$\n\n**Note**: For the original family, since $C\\ne 0,$ we have $y\\ne 0$ whenever $x\\ne 0,$ hence, the slopes are well-defined. Otherwise, when $x=0,$ the curve is not defined." 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363903 1075 #edb/ref 17592186363904 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363903 1075 #edb/ref 17592186363905 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363903 1075 #edb/ref 17592186363906 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363903 1075 #edb/ref 17592186363907 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363903 1075