1 "Determine the differential equation satisfied by the orthogonal trajectories of the family of curves ${y}^{2}=-\\frac{3C}{{x}^{2}},$ where $C\\ne 0$ is a parameter, in terms of $x$ and $y$ only." 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363909 1072 #uuid "8d4d5768-937a-5096-afaf-5a1645a9f0c8" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363909 1074 "Orthogonal trajectories are families of curves that intersect another given family of curves at right angles. In other words, the tangent lines of curves from each family are perpendicular at every intersection point.\n\nLet's determine the slope of the family of curves given by ${y}^{2}=-\\frac{3C}{{x}^{2}}.$ First, we implicitly differentiate with respect to $x$ and solve for the derivative of $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{d}{dx}({y}^{2}) & =\\frac{d}{dx}\\,(-\\frac{3C}{{x}^{2}}) \\\\ 2y{y}^{′} & =\\frac{6C}{{x}^{3}} \\\\ {y}^{′} & =\\frac{3C}{{x}^{3}y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we must eliminate $C.$ From the original family of curves equation, we have\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}=-\\frac{3C}{{x}^{2}}\\,⟹\\,C=-\\frac{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}}{3}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into our slope equation, we get\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\frac{3(-\\frac{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}}{3})}{{x}^{3}y}=-\\frac{y}{x}.\n$$\n\nFor a curve to intersect this family orthogonally, its slope ${y}_{⊥}^{′}$ must be the negative reciprocal of the slope ${y}^{′}$ above. Hence,\n\n$$\n{y}_{⊥}^{′}=-\\frac{1}{{y}^{′}}=-\\frac{1}{(-\\frac{y}{x})}=\\frac{x}{y}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the orthogonal trajectories must satisfy\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{x}{y}.\n$$\n\n**Note**: For the original family, since $C\\ne 0,$ we have $y\\ne 0$ whenever $x\\ne 0,$ hence, the slopes are well-defined. Otherwise, when $x=0,$ the curve is not defined." 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363910 1075 #edb/ref 17592186363911 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363910 1075 #edb/ref 17592186363912 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363910 1075 #edb/ref 17592186363913 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363910 1075 #edb/ref 17592186363914 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363910 1075 #edb/ref 17592186363915 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363910 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363910 1077 "selection" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363910 1079 #edb/ref 17592186363912 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363910 1080 #uuid "2e5a7bf9-7ed7-5f8d-ae5a-28a81c6b563d" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363911 1081 "\\frac{dy}{dx}=-\\frac{x}{y}" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363911 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363911 1084 #uuid "0ec044b6-90fe-559a-a74c-8c1d516f1d3d" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363912 1081 "\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{x}{y}" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363912 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363912 1084 #uuid "c7063a98-b14b-553b-9bfb-cf179c08a333" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363913 1081 "\\frac{dy}{dx}=-\\frac{y}{x}" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363913 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363913 1084 #uuid "1de77dd8-d543-5789-9a42-79d550c52e7f" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363914 1081 "\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{{y}^{2}}{x}" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363914 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363914 1084 #uuid "90d12a8a-d619-544c-845e-eba3e402f696" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363915 1081 "\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{y}{x}" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363915 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363915 1084 #uuid "bf969a54-d43a-57cd-ae16-f37e51952fc5" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363916 1068 #edb/ref 17592186363917 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363916 1069 "q-337567" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363916 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363916 1071 "Find the orthogonal trajectories of the family of curves ${e}^{x}=y-C,$ where $C$ and $K$ are parameters." 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363916 1072 #uuid "52f3f0d4-9988-5b9b-b9ad-72185238017c" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363916 1074 "Orthogonal trajectories are families of curves that intersect another given family of curves at right angles. In other words, the tangent lines of curves from each family are perpendicular at every intersection point.\n\nLet's determine the slope of the family of curves given by ${e}^{x}=y-C.$ First, we implicitly differentiate with respect to $x$ and solve for the derivative of $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{d}{dx}\\,({e}^{x}) & =\\frac{d}{dx}(y-C) \\\\ {e}^{x} & ={y}^{′} \\\\ {y}^{′} & ={e}^{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that the parameter $C$ has already been eliminated.\n\nFor a curve to intersect this family orthogonally, its slope ${y}_{⊥}^{′}$ must be the negative reciprocal of the slope ${y}^{′}$ above. Hence,\n\n$$\n{y}_{⊥}^{′}=-\\frac{1}{{y}^{′}}=-\\frac{1}{({e}^{x})}=-{e}^{-x}.\n$$\n\nHence, the orthogonal trajectories must satisfy\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=-{e}^{-x}.\n$$\n\nTo find the general solution, we separate the variables and integrate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx} & =-{e}^{-x} \\\\ \\int \\frac{dy}{dx}\\,dx & =\\int (-{e}^{-x})\\,dx \\\\ \\int 1\\,dy & =\\int (-{e}^{-x})\\,dx \\\\ y & ={e}^{-x}+K\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $K$ is a constant of integration.\n\nTherefore, the orthogonal trajectories of the family of curves ${e}^{x}=y-C,$ where $C$ is a parameter, are given by\n\n$$\ny={e}^{-x}+K,\n$$\n\nwhere $K$ is a parameter. Some curves from each family for specific parameter values are given in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/b8/b8ce7390522ca1ba25635d029bdcea9dcec913d4b97a93ff18b5dd5dd6d02834.png)\n\n**Note**: For the slopes ${y}^{′}$ and ${y}_{⊥}^{′},$ we divide by ${y}^{′}$ and ${e}^{x},$ respectively. So, the orthogonal trajectories are only defined on the region where ${e}^{x}\\ne 0.$" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363917 1075 #edb/ref 17592186363918 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363917 1075 #edb/ref 17592186363919 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363917 1075 #edb/ref 17592186363920 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363917 1075 #edb/ref 17592186363921 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363917 1075 #edb/ref 17592186363922 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363917 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363917 1077 "selection" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363917 1079 #edb/ref 17592186363922 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363917 1080 #uuid "e522c6cb-f9d6-5987-ad61-528a8ff7da2c" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363918 1081 "{x}^{2}y=K" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363918 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363918 1084 #uuid "9b973018-021e-5257-9f64-7b1dca8f2936" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363919 1081 "x=Ky" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363919 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363919 1084 #uuid "3f074d25-df8c-5f17-9004-9d4819ded463" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363920 1081 "{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=K" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363920 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363920 1084 #uuid "fbdd289a-1e29-5208-9614-ec518ec0edcb" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363921 1081 "y=Kx" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363921 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363921 1084 #uuid "438897e7-ffe3-5941-b12d-caf4ad481d23" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363922 1081 "y={e}^{-x}+K" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363922 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363922 1084 #uuid "7eaf5a30-ace3-555d-865a-9cf9c37b4163" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363923 1068 #edb/ref 17592186363924 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363923 1069 "q-337569" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363923 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363923 1071 "Find the orthogonal trajectories of the family of curves ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=C,$ where $C$ and $K$ are parameters." 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363923 1072 #uuid "20e84823-52da-5d8d-9751-7e5bcade7fd3" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363923 1074 "Orthogonal trajectories are families of curves that intersect another given family of curves at right angles. In other words, the tangent lines of curves from each family are perpendicular at every intersection point.\n\nLet's determine the slope of the family of curves given by ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=C.$ First, we implicitly differentiate with respect to $x$ and solve for the derivative of $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{d}{dx}\\,({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}) & =\\frac{d}{dx}(C) \\\\ 2x+2y{y}^{′} & =0 \\\\ {y}^{′} & =-\\frac{x}{y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that the parameter $C$ has already been eliminated.\n\nFor a curve to intersect this family orthogonally, its slope ${y}_{⊥}^{′}$ must be the negative reciprocal of the slope ${y}^{′}$ above. Hence,\n\n$$\n{y}_{⊥}^{′}=-\\frac{1}{{y}^{′}}=-\\frac{1}{(-\\frac{x}{y})}=\\frac{y}{x}.\n$$\n\nHence, the orthogonal trajectories must satisfy\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{y}{x}.\n$$\n\nTo find the general solution, we separate the variables and integrate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{y}\\cdot \\frac{dy}{dx} & =\\frac{1}{x} \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{y}\\cdot \\frac{dy}{dx}\\,dx & =\\int \\frac{1}{x}\\,dx \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{y}\\,dy & =\\int \\frac{1}{x}\\,dx \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|y| & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|+{K}_{1} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|y|-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x| & ={K}_{1} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\frac{y}{x}| & ={K}_{1} \\\\ \\frac{y}{x} & =K \\\\ y & =Kx\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $K=\\pm {e}^{{K}_{1}}$ is a constant of integration.\n\nTherefore, the orthogonal trajectories of the family of curves ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=C,$ where $C$ is a parameter, are given by\n\n$$\ny=Kx,\n$$\n\nwhere $K$ is a parameter. Some curves from each family for specific parameter values are given in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/ca/ca980af3c5db940a9ab95567dfaa1a69ea8afb9f4f42f772bb446df67df129b8.png)\n\n**Note**: For the slopes ${y}^{′}$ and ${y}_{⊥}^{′},$ we divide by $y$ and $x,$ respectively. So, the orthogonal trajectories are only defined on the region $x,y\\ne 0.$" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363924 1075 #edb/ref 17592186363925 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363924 1075 #edb/ref 17592186363926 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363924 1075 #edb/ref 17592186363927 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363924 1075 #edb/ref 17592186363928 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363924 1075 #edb/ref 17592186363929 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363924 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363924 1077 "selection" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363924 1079 #edb/ref 17592186363928 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363924 1080 #uuid "ba512409-31cf-5e9d-99b6-4d1f84e3bf75" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363925 1081 "{x}^{2}y=K" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363925 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363925 1084 #uuid "833addb7-0164-5f7c-bb34-29651996fbc7" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363926 1081 "x=Ky" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363926 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363926 1084 #uuid "bb7ee723-e1fc-538d-a9fe-fe42e7426afa" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363927 1081 "{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=K" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363927 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363927 1084 #uuid "75811765-47db-5662-b560-125a09e37856" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363928 1081 "y=Kx" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363928 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363928 1084 #uuid "e791fc1a-5891-5df4-bee0-fa25c7da72b9" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363929 1081 "xy=K" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363929 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363929 1084 #uuid "1faceebb-b201-5935-b797-b0dcbc04a0d7" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363930 1068 #edb/ref 17592186363931 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363930 1069 "q-337577" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363930 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363930 1071 "Find the orthogonal trajectories of the family of curves $y={x}^{2}+C,$ where $C$ and $K$ are parameters." 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363930 1072 #uuid "95e7abe8-3e12-5829-b71d-e930b03de0fe" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363930 1074 "Orthogonal trajectories are families of curves that intersect another given family of curves at right angles. In other words, the tangent lines of curves from each family are perpendicular at every intersection point.\n\nLet's determine the slope of the family of curves given by $y={x}^{2}+C.$ First, we implicitly differentiate with respect to $x$ and solve for the derivative of $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{d}{dx}(y) & =\\frac{d}{dx}\\,({x}^{2}+C) \\\\ {y}^{′} & =2x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that the parameter $C$ has already been eliminated.\n\nFor a curve to intersect this family orthogonally, its slope ${y}_{⊥}^{′}$ must be the negative reciprocal of the slope ${y}^{′}$ above. Hence,\n\n$$\n{y}_{⊥}^{′}=-\\frac{1}{{y}^{′}}=-\\frac{1}{(2x)}=-\\frac{1}{2x}.\n$$\n\nHence, the orthogonal trajectories must satisfy\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=-\\frac{1}{2x}.\n$$\n\nTo find the general solution, we separate the variables and integrate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx} & =-\\frac{1}{2x} \\\\ \\int \\frac{dy}{dx}\\,dx & =\\int (-\\frac{1}{2x})\\,dx \\\\ \\int 1\\,dy & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\int \\frac{1}{x}\\,dx \\\\ y & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|+K\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $K$ is a constant of integration.\n\nTherefore, the orthogonal trajectories of the family of curves $y={x}^{2}+C,$ where $C$ is a parameter, are given by\n\n$$\ny=-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|+K,\n$$\n\nwhere $K$ is a parameter. Some curves from each family for specific parameter values are given in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/37/37019d0c75ba56e28d3e2a592b806941b16005ddb906f4aa126547c0925bd14c.png)\n\n**Note**: For the slopes ${y}^{′}$ and ${y}_{⊥}^{′},$ we divide by $x$ when finding ${y}_{⊥}^{′}.$ So, the orthogonal trajectories are only defined on the region $x\\ne 0.$" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363931 1075 #edb/ref 17592186363932 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363931 1075 #edb/ref 17592186363933 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363931 1075 #edb/ref 17592186363934 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363931 1075 #edb/ref 17592186363935 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363931 1075 #edb/ref 17592186363936 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363931 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363931 1077 "selection" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363931 1079 #edb/ref 17592186363936 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363931 1080 #uuid "4e59c1a4-b0c0-53ec-bffd-738ded8226df" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363932 1081 "x=Ky" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363932 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363932 1084 #uuid "0ffd8ebb-c265-553a-930f-4c55b4b9f184" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363933 1081 "{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=K" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363933 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363933 1084 #uuid "e210cc25-c13f-5e25-9440-1c76880dc514" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363934 1081 "y=Kx" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363934 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363934 1084 #uuid "d062e833-85cc-5d6f-b2a9-8e7780d807a1" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363935 1081 "xy=K" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363935 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363935 1084 #uuid "d23e1857-def3-5b34-be92-44a516f342f6" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363936 1081 "y=-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|+K" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363936 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363936 1084 #uuid "97f12d19-6d42-5216-844d-fac39e4f2bb7" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363937 1068 #edb/ref 17592186363938 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363937 1069 "q-337598" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363937 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363937 1071 "Find the orthogonal trajectories of the family of curves ${y}^{2}=Cx,$ where $C\\ne 0$ and $K$ are parameters." 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363937 1072 #uuid "1112c629-7ea8-5085-813a-086cecee9fd0" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363937 1074 "Orthogonal trajectories are families of curves that intersect another given family of curves at right angles. In other words, the tangent lines of curves from each family are perpendicular at every intersection point.\n\nLet's determine the slope of the family of curves given by ${y}^{2}=Cx.$ First, we implicitly differentiate with respect to $x$ and solve for the derivative of $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{d}{dx}({y}^{2}) & =\\frac{d}{dx}(Cx) \\\\ 2y{y}^{′} & =C \\\\ {y}^{′} & =\\frac{C}{2y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we must eliminate $C.$ From the original family of curves equation, we have\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}=Cx\\,⟹\\,C=\\frac{{y}^{2}}{x}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into our slope equation, we get\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\frac{(\\frac{{y}^{2}}{x})}{2y}=\\frac{y}{2x}.\n$$\n\nFor a curve to intersect this family orthogonally, its slope ${y}_{⊥}^{′}$ must be the negative reciprocal of the slope ${y}^{′}$ above. Hence,\n\n$$\n{y}_{⊥}^{′}=-\\frac{1}{{y}^{′}}=-\\frac{1}{(\\frac{y}{2x})}=-\\frac{2x}{y}.\n$$\n\nHence, the orthogonal trajectories must satisfy\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=-\\frac{2x}{y}.\n$$\n\nTo find the general solution, we separate the variables and integrate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y\\cdot \\frac{dy}{dx} & =-2x \\\\ \\int y\\cdot \\frac{dy}{dx}\\,dx & =\\int (-2x)\\,dx \\\\ \\int y\\,dy & =-2\\int x\\,dx \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{2} & =-{x}^{2}+K \\\\ {y}^{2}+2{x}^{2} & =K\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $K$ is a constant of integration.\n\nTherefore, the orthogonal trajectories of the family of curves ${y}^{2}=Cx,$ where $C$ is a parameter, are given by\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}+2{x}^{2}=K,\n$$\n\nwhere $K$ is a parameter. Some curves from each family for specific parameter values are given in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/03/039a42b728cad07b3e8f5dfd2f1607b36819e586f40e54e06d942679bb4ededa.png)\n\n**Note**: Since $C\\ne 0,$ we have $y\\ne 0$ on every curve in the family (except possibly at $x=0$), so slopes ${y}^{′}$ and ${}_{⊥}^{′}$ are well-defined away from $y=0.$" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363938 1075 #edb/ref 17592186363939 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363938 1075 #edb/ref 17592186363940 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363938 1075 #edb/ref 17592186