atorname{cos}⁡x+{u}_{2}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=0" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363998 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363998 1084 #uuid "50ec3dc4-9957-5dd4-bf06-1c7a9b4da598" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363999 1068 #edb/ref 17592186364000 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363999 1069 "q-340344" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363999 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363999 1071 "Which of the following statements is true regarding the equilibrium solutions of ${y}^{′}=(y-3)({y}^{2}+5)?$" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363999 1072 #uuid "b343081e-f618-50fb-8cd3-8bac934f3e0f" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186363999 1074 "We can classify the equilibrium solutions of an autonomous ODE ${y}^{′}=f(y)$ by examining its phase line.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *sink* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line both point *toward* the equilibrium solution.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *source* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line both point *away* from the equilibrium solution.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *node* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line point in the same direction.\n\nFirst, we need to find the equilibrium solutions. So, we solve $f(y)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(y) & =0 \\\\ (y-3)({y}^{2}+5) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the equilibrium solution is $y=3.$ \n\nNow, we analyze the sign of $f(y)$ using a sign table, as follows:\n\n|  | $(-\\infty ,3)$ | $(3,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $y-3$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n| ${y}^{2}+5$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $f(y)$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n\nSo, we have that $f(y)$ is positive for $y\\in (3,\\infty ),$ and negative for $y\\in (-\\infty ,3).$\n\nWe can now draw the phase line for this equation.\n\n![](images/bb/bb9204b3eb9bcf7fe536508f5635356aacdbd8e29f276b03bac9dddd916609bb.png)\n\nFinally, we classify the equilibrium solution:\n\n- $y=3$ is a source because the nearby solutions diverge away from this point.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$y=3$ is a source\"." 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364000 1075 #edb/ref 17592186364001 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364000 1075 #edb/ref 17592186364002 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364000 1075 #edb/ref 17592186364003 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364000 1075 #edb/ref 17592186364004 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364000 1075 #edb/ref 17592186364005 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364000 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364000 1077 "selection" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364000 1079 #edb/ref 17592186364005 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364000 1080 #uuid "d6858d42-6ccf-559c-a286-454ea7a3acd2" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364001 1081 "$y=3$ is a node" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364001 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045500 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364001 1084 #uuid "01b51afd-17cc-5f52-afd7-77de192930a6" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364002 1081 "$y=3$ is a source and $y=-3$ is a sink" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364002 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045500 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364002 1084 #uuid "61b64586-d698-5841-adf5-803349002047" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364003 1081 "$y=3$ is a sink" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364003 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045500 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364003 1084 #uuid "b9a800f7-3027-5549-993a-ce836a2294ac" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364004 1081 "$y=3$ is a sink and $y=-3$ is a source" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364004 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045500 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364004 1084 #uuid "e135b204-c1bc-5ec5-950b-2e613adeb18b" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364005 1081 "$y=3$ is a source" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364005 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045500 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364005 1084 #uuid "2d98202e-2b70-5bc6-b94a-b9e318f37f2f" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364006 1068 #edb/ref 17592186364007 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364006 1068 #edb/ref 17592186364014 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364006 1069 "q-341125" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364006 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364006 1071 "Use the Laplace transform of $f(t)={e}^{kt}$ to find the Laplace transform of $\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(5t).$$\\,L{\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(5t)}=$ {{field-1}},\ndefined for {{field-2}}." 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364006 1072 #uuid "3dbc92cb-1339-57fe-b1ef-0f6893134b4e" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364006 1074 "The linearity property of the Laplace transform states that for functions $f(t)$ and $g(t),$ we have\n\n$$\nL{\\alpha f(t)+\\beta g(t)}=\\alpha \\cdot L{f(t)}+\\beta \\cdot L{g(t)},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants.\n\nFirst, recall that, by definition\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(5t)=\\frac{{e}^{5t}-{e}^{-5t}}{2}.\n$$\n\nTaking the Laplace transform and applying the linearity property, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(5t)} & =L{\\frac{{e}^{5t}-{e}^{-5t}}{2}} \\\\  & =L{\\frac{{e}^{5t}}{2}-\\frac{{e}^{-5t}}{2}} \\\\  & =L{\\frac{{e}^{5t}}{2}}-L{\\frac{{e}^{-5t}}{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}L{{e}^{5t}}-\\frac{1}{2}L{{e}^{-5t}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we recall that \n\n$$\nL{{e}^{kt}}=\\frac{1}{s-k},\\,s>k.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{{e}^{5t}} & =\\frac{1}{s-5},\\,s>5 \\\\ L{{e}^{-5t}} & =\\frac{1}{s+5},\\,s>-5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Laplace transform of $\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(5t)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(5t)} & =\\frac{1}{2}L{{e}^{5t}}-\\frac{1}{2}L{{e}^{-5t}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{s-5}-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{s+5} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{1}{s-5}-\\frac{1}{s+5}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{(s+5)-(s-5)}{(s-5)(s+5)}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{10}{{s}^{2}-25}) \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{{s}^{2}-25}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since $L{{e}^{5t}}$ is defined for $s\\in (5,\\infty )$ and $L{{e}^{-5t}}$ is defined for $s\\in (-5,\\infty ),$ we have that $L{\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(5t)}$ is defined for\n\n$$\n(5,\\infty )\\cap (-5,\\infty )=(5,\\infty ),\n$$\n\nthat is, $s>5.$" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364007 1075 #edb/ref 17592186364008 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364007 1075 #edb/ref 17592186364009 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364007 1075 #edb/ref 17592186364010 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364007 1075 #edb/ref 17592186364011 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364007 1075 #edb/ref 17592186364012 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364007 1075 #edb/ref 17592186364013 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364007 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045494 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364007 1077 "field-2" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364007 1079 #edb/ref 17592186364009 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364007 1080 #uuid "9ebbcb18-2090-5c68-b6a4-04c751298594" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364008 1081 "s\\ge -5" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364008 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364008 1084 #uuid "0a615dd0-3f77-5dfd-b26b-39f0cde70998" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364009 1081 "s>5" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364009 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364009 1084 #uuid "8f291ac4-6543-5679-93d1-65e628f4e1ec" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364010 1081 "s\\ge 5" 13194139533445 175921860