\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =-48.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the volume of the cube is decreasing at a rate of $48\\,{\\text{cm}}^{3}/\\text{s}$ at the moment when $x=2\\,\\text{cm}.$" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364036 1075 #edb/ref 17592186364037 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364036 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364036 1077 "field-1" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364036 1079 #edb/ref 17592186364037 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364036 1080 #uuid "5d0dba7a-cc6c-590a-abe1-04aed8376455" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364037 1081 "48" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364037 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364037 1084 #uuid "78304fee-412a-521d-a385-f33fcf488eb9" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364038 1068 #edb/ref 17592186364039 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364038 1069 "q-343325" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364038 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364038 1071 "![](images/57/574730352afe4f78c83ae9755df8dbe362a3699000955d17d2d907fa3e842b98.png)\n\nA bar of soap wearing down with use gradually decreases in all its dimensions. Its height is decreasing at a rate of $0.05\\,\\text{cm/s},$ its width is decreasing at a rate of $0.1\\,\\text{cm/s},$ and its length is decreasing at a rate of $0.2\\,\\text{cm/s}.$ How fast is its volume changing when its dimensions are $12\\,\\text{cm}$ in length, $5\\,\\text{cm}$ in width, and $4\\,\\text{cm}$ in height? Assume that the rate of decrease of volume is positive.\n\nThe volume of the bar of soap is decreasing at a rate of\n{{field-1}}\n${\\text{cm}}^{3}\\text{/s}.$" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364038 1072 #uuid "6812daeb-0545-51ac-ad16-eba349aac5cb" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364038 1074 "Let $L,$ $W,$ $H$ and $V$ be the length, the width, the height, and the volume of the box, respectively. We know that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L=12\\text{ cm},\\, & W=5\\text{ cm}, \\\\ H=4\\text{ cm},\\, & \\frac{\\text{d}L}{\\text{d}t}=-0.2\\text{ cm/s}, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}W}{\\text{d}t}=-0.1\\text{ cm/s},\\, & \\frac{\\text{d}H}{\\text{d}t}=-0.05\\text{ cm/s}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe would like to find $\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}.$\n\nThe relation between the four variables is given by the formula for the volume of a rectangular prism:\n\n$$\nV=LWH\n$$\n\nWe differentiate both sides of $V=LWH$ with respect to $t,$ using the product rule, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}V & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(LWH) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =W\\cdot H(\\frac{\\text{d}L}{\\text{d}t})+L\\cdot H(\\frac{\\text{d}W}{\\text{d}t})+L\\cdot W(\\frac{\\text{d}H}{\\text{d}t}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can plug in the known values and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =(5)(4)(-0.2)+(12)(4)(-0.1)+(12)(5)(-0.05) \\\\  & =-11.8{\\text{ cm}}^{3}/\\text{s}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the volume of the bar of soap is decreasing at a rate of $11.8{\\text{ cm}}^{3}/\\text{s}.$" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364039 1075 #edb/ref 17592186364040 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364039 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364039 1077 "field-1" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364039 1079 #edb/ref 17592186364040 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364039 1080 #uuid "73936d24-8096-5da1-807d-53eace93737a" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364040 1081 "11.8" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364040 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364040 1084 #uuid "9387c868-c63f-5bf3-b005-dbb97af1df43" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364041 1068 #edb/ref 17592186364042 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364041 1069 "q-343349" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364041 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364041 1071 "![](images/6e/6e41308fb734cdb42fe0d199a46e7db8edd25bd5d9ea5a3fabff0601a42dce51.png)\n\nA rectangular loaf of rising bread dough is squeezed between two rigid side panels that keep its width fixed at $60\\,\\text{mm}.$ The length of the loaf is decreasing by $2\\,\\text{mm}\\text{/s}$ and its height is increasing by $1\\,\\text{mm}\\text{/s}.$ How fast is its volume changing when its dimensions are $100\\,\\text{mm}$ in length and $30\\,\\text{mm}$ in height?\n\nThe volume is increasing at a rate of\n{{field-1}}\n${\\text{mm}}^{3}/\\text{s}.$" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364041 1072 #uuid "010f8799-9b1d-5084-ad88-8118957f7d57" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364041 1074 "We define the following variables:\n\n- let $L,$ $W,$ and $H$ be the length, width, and height of the bread loaf, respectively, and\n\n- let $V$ be the volume of the bread loaf.\n\nNote that $L,$ $H,$ and $V$ change with time, while $W$ is constant. \n\nWe are given that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L=100\\text{ mm},\\, & W=60\\text{ mm}, \\\\ H=30\\text{ mm},\\, & \\frac{\\text{d}L}{\\text{d}t}=-2\\text{ mm/s}, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}H}{\\text{d}t}=1\\text{ mm/s}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe would like to find $\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}.$\n\nThe relation between the three time-dependent variables, $V,$ $H$ and $L,$ is given by the formula for the volume of bread loaf:\n\n$$\nV=LWH\n$$\n\nWe differentiate both sides of $V=LWH$ with respect to $t,$ treating $W$ as a constant, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}V & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(LWH) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =W\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(LH) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =W[L(\\frac{\\text{d}H}{\\text{d}t})+H(\\frac{\\text{d}L}{\\text{d}t})] \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =W\\cdot L(\\frac{\\text{d}H}{\\text{d}t})+W\\cdot H(\\frac{\\text{d}L}{\\text{d}t}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can plug in the known values and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =(60)(100)(1)+(60)(30)(-2) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =6000-3600 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =2400.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the volume of bread loaf is increasing at a rate of $2400\\,{\\text{mm}}^{3}\\text{/s}.$" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364042 1075 #edb/ref 17592186364043 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364042 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364042 1077 "field-1" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364042 1079 #edb/ref 17592186364043 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364042 1080 #uuid "c83b4c80-e4d4-5493-b84f-95e022ee40b2" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364043 1081 "2400" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364043 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364043 1084 #uuid "8b5eb1c8-01c9-5671-a035-b45ec2547eb3" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364044 1068 #edb/ref 17592186364045 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364044 1069 "q-343366" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364044 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364044 1071 "$$\nF(x)={\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-26, & x\\in [-5,0) \\\\ -3x, & x\\in [0,5)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the function $F(x)$ shown above. Find the value to which the Fourier series of the $10$-periodic extension $f(x)$ of $F(x)$ converges when $x=5.$\n\nThe Fourier series converges to {{field-1}} when $x=5.$" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364044 1072 #uuid "ae903844-3154-5fe9-a420-6a9e8272ce91" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364044 1074 "Suppose that $f(x)$ is a $2L$-periodic piecewise smooth function with the corresponding Fourier series\n\n$$\nS(x)=\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi }{L}x)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi }{L}x).\n$$\n\nThen, the series converges for all $x\\in R.$\n\n- If $f(x)$ is continuous at $x,$ then the series converges to the value of the function at this point:\n\n$$\nS(x)=f(x)\n$$\n\n- If $f(x)$ has a jump discontinuity at $x,$ then the series converges to the midpoint between the values of left- and right-sided limits of the function at this point:\n\n$$\nS(x)=\\frac{{f}^{-}(x)+{f}^{+}(x)}{2}\n$$\n\nwhere ${f}^{-}(x)$ and ${f}^{+}(x)$ are the left- and right-sided limits of the function at this point, respectively.\n\nNotice that the $10$-periodic extension $f(x)$ of our function $F(x)$ is not continuous at $x=5.$ The one-sided limits at this point are the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{-}(5) & ={lim}_{x\\to {5}^{-}}f(x)=-3(5)=-15 \\\\ {f}^{+}(5) & ={lim}_{x\\to {5}^{+}}f(x)={5}^{2}-26=-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the Fourier series of $f(x)$ converges to \n\n$$\n\\frac{{f}^{-}(5)+{f}^{+}(5)}{2}=\\frac{-15+(-1)}{2}=-8.\n$$" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364045 1075 #edb/ref 17592186364046 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364045 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364045 1077 "field-1" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364045 1079 #edb/ref 17592186364046 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364045 1080 #uuid "948a4c6b-6866-5b53-840b-620e511c4ece" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364046 1081 "-8" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364046 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364046 1084 #uuid "4dcce98c-8760-56f2-b17b-5fd945b150e4" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364047 1068 #edb/ref 17592186364048 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364047 1069 "q-343367" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364047 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364047 1071 "$$\nF(x)={\\begin{aligned}2, & x\\in [-\\pi ,0) \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡x, & x\\in [0,\\pi )\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the function $F(x)$ shown above. Find the value to which the Fourier series of the $2\\pi $-periodic extension $f(x)$ of $F(x)$ converges when $x=\\pi .$\n\nThe Fourier series converges to {{field-1}} when $x=\\pi .$" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364047 1072 #uuid "8508b9f1-644b-5b60-9272-323a48701a7e" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364047 1074 "Suppose that $f(x)$ is a $2L$-periodic piecewise smooth function with the corresponding Fourier series\n\n$$\nS(x)=\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi }{L}x)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi }{L}x).\n$$\n\nThen, the series converges for all $x\\in R.$\n\n- If $f(x)$ is continuous at $x,$ then the series converges to the value of the function at this point:\n\n$$\nS(x)=f(x)\n$$\n\n- If $f(x)$ has a jump discontinuity at $x,$ then the series converges to the midpoint between the values of left- and right-sided limits of the function at this point:\n\n$$\nS(x)=\\frac{{f}^{-}(x)+{f}^{+}(x)}{2}\n$$\n\nwhere ${f}^{-}(x)$ and ${f}^{+}(x)$ are the left- and right-sided limits of the function at this point, respectively.\n\nNotice that the $2\\pi $-periodic extension $f(x)$ of our function $F(x)$ is not continuous at $x=\\pi .$ The one-sided limits at this point are the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{-}(\\pi ) & ={lim}_{x\\to {\\pi }^{-}}f(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi =0 \\\\ {f}^{+}(\\pi ) & ={lim}_{x\\to {\\pi }^{+}}f(x)=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the Fourier series of $f(x)$ converges to \n\n$$\n\\frac{{f}^{-}(\\pi )+{f}^{+}(\\pi )}{2}=\\frac{0+2}{2}=1.\n$$" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364048 1075 #edb/ref 17592186364049 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364048 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364048 1077 "field-1" 13194139533445 175921860455