in{aligned}{f}^{-}\\,(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & ={lim}_{x\\to (\\pi /2)^{-}}f(x)=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=2 \\\\ {f}^{+}\\,(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & ={lim}_{x\\to (\\pi /2)^{+}}f(x)=-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the Fourier series of $f(x)$ converges to \n\n$$\n\\frac{{f}^{-}\\,(\\frac{\\pi }{2})+{f}^{+}\\,(\\frac{\\pi }{2})}{2}=\\frac{2+(-4)}{2}=-1.\n$$" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364069 1075 #edb/ref 17592186364070 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364069 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364069 1077 "field-1" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364069 1079 #edb/ref 17592186364070 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364069 1080 #uuid "05225483-6672-597f-a26b-7bc809c08b7c" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364070 1081 "-1" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364070 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364070 1084 #uuid "e2acba6b-85a0-5271-998e-5cb3d652c89e" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364071 1068 #edb/ref 17592186364072 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364071 1069 "q-346863" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364071 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364071 1071 "A particle moves along a straight line relative to a fixed origin $O$ with velocity $v(t)={sec}^{2}⁡(t),$ where $t>0$ is the time. If the particle is at the position $x=2$ when $t=\\frac{\\pi }{4},$ calculate the position $x$ of the particle at time $t.$\n\n$x(t)=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364071 1072 #uuid "1d07345a-3a0a-518b-82c7-108ea7e7359c" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364071 1074 "We start by integrating the velocity to get $x(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =\\int v(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int {sec}^{2}⁡(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡(t)+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C$, we use the fact that $x(\\frac{\\pi }{4})=2.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2 & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})+C \\\\ 2 & =1+C \\\\ C & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the position $x$ of the particle at time $t$ is \n\n$$\nx(t)=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(t)+1.\n$$" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364072 1075 #edb/ref 17592186364073 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364072 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364072 1077 "field-1" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364072 1079 #edb/ref 17592186364073 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364072 1080 #uuid "076095c7-fee4-5daa-9aa5-38104c357d45" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364073 1081 "\\operatorname{tan}(t)+1" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364073 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364073 1084 #uuid "45779598-424d-5b7c-b0dc-1b9dbdc494f6" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364074 1068 #edb/ref 17592186364075 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364074 1069 "q-346866" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364074 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364074 1071 "A particle moves along a straight line relative to a fixed origin $O$ with acceleration $a(t)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t),$ where $t\\ge 0$ is the time. If the particle has position $x=0$ and velocity $v=1$ when $t=0,$ the position $x$ of the particle at time $t$ is\n\n$x(t)=$\n{{field-1}}." 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364074 1072 #uuid "f58af2a1-073c-5f4c-87d5-1658e07ab17d" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364074 1074 "We start by integrating the acceleration to get the velocity $v(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C,$ we use the fact that $v(0)=1.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1 & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)+C \\\\ 1 & =-\\frac{1}{2}+C \\\\ C & =\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the velocity is given by $v(t)=-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\frac{3}{2}.$\n\nNow, we integrate the velocity to get $x(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =\\int v(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int (-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\frac{3}{2})\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\,\\text{d}t+\\frac{3}{2}\\int \\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)+\\frac{3}{2}t+K\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $K$, we use the fact that $x(0)=0.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0 & =-\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)+\\frac{3}{2}(0)+K \\\\ K & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the position $x$ of the particle at time $t$ is \n\n$$\nx(t)=-\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)+\\frac{3}{2}t.\n$$" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364075 1075 #edb/ref 17592186364076 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364075 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364075 1077 "field-1" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364075 1079 #edb/ref 17592186364076 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364075 1080 #uuid "1fc5eb32-bd14-5550-b830-a9aef3837e01" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364076 1081 "-\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{sin}(2t)+\\frac{3}{2}t" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364076 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364076 1084 #uuid "a6658038-57c3-5b1e-a0e8-5c2429b7e472" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364077 1068 #edb/ref 17592186364078 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364077 1069 "q-350209" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364077 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364077 1071 "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{1}^{e}\\frac{3\\sqrt{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}}{x}\\,\\text{d}x=$ \n{{field-1}}" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364077 1072 #uuid "7909088b-942e-5c28-83d7-5489395899d0" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364077 1074 "Let's set $u=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x.$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{x}\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}u=\\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nCalculating the limits for $u$ gives the following:\n\n| $x$ | $1$ | $e$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $0$ | $1$ |\n\nWe now substitute and evaluate as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{e}\\frac{3\\sqrt{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}}{x}\\,\\text{d}x & =3{\\int }_{1}^{e}\\sqrt{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}\\cdot \\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\sqrt{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =3{\\int }_{0}^{1}{u}^{1/2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =3\\cdot \\frac{{u}^{3/2}}{3/2}\\,{|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =2\\cdot {u}^{3/2}\\,{|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =2({1}^{3/2}-{0}^{3/2}) \\\\  & =2(1-0) \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364078 1075 #edb/ref 17592186364079 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364078 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364078 1077 "field-1" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364078 1079 #edb/ref 17592186364079 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364078 1080 #uuid "49d84293-79fe-5d8a-8714-af649008e9a9" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364079 1081 "2" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364079 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364079 1084 #uuid "e7f5c99e-9a37-5820-a1ca-cf048d293561" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364080 1068 #edb/ref 17592186364081 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364080 1069 "q-351282" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364080 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364080 1071 "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{1}^{e}\\frac{4\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{3})}{5x}\\,\\text{d}x=$ \n{{field-1}}" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364080 1072 #uuid "a467975c-198b-531b-975f-70d1e6e0a17e" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364080 1074 "Let's set $u=\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{3}).$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}}\\cdot 3{x}^{2}=\\frac{3}{x}\\,⟹\\,\\frac{1}{3}\\text{d}u=\\frac{\\text{d}x}{x}.\n$$\n\nCalculating the limits for $u$ gives the following:\n\n| $x$ | $1$ | $e$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $0$ | $3$ |\n\nWe now substitute and evaluate as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{e}\\frac{4\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{3})}{5x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{4}{5}{\\int }_{1}^{e}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{3})\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{x} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{5}{\\int }_{0}^{3}u\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{15}{\\int }_{0}^{3}u\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{15}\\cdot {\\frac{{u}^{2}}{2}|}_{0}^{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{15}\\cdot \\frac{9}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364081 1075 #edb/ref 17592186364082 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364081 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364081 1077 "field-1" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364081 1079 #edb/ref 17592186364082 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364081 1080 #uuid "b4f0da75-3954-585b-bedf-4f0ec53da429" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364082 1081 "\\frac{6}{5}" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364082 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364082 1084 #uuid "9b922c00-fec0-5e92-b344-12d9b0a9d88e" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364083 1068 #edb/ref 17592186364084 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364083 1069 "q-351286" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364083 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364083 1071 "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{2}^{8}\\frac{{log}_{2}⁡(2x)}{x}\\,\\text{d}x=($\n{{field-1}}\n$)\\operatorname{ln}⁡2$" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364083 1072 #uuid "1a82fa79-cd38-52c8-bdda-6f1fd4a33aa2" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364083 1074 "Let $u={log}_{2}⁡(2x).$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}\\cdot 2=\\frac{1}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}\\,⟹\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡2\\,\\text{d}u=\\frac{\\text{d}x}{x}.\n$$\n\nCalculating the limits for $u$ gives the following:\n\n| $x$ | $2$ | $8$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $2$ | $4$ |\n\nWe now substitute and evaluate as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{2}^{8}\\frac{{log}_{2}⁡(2x)}{x}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{2}^{8}{log}_{2}⁡(2x)\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{x} \\\\  & ={\\int }_{2}^{4}u\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡2\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡2{\\int }_{2}^{4}u\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡2\\cdot \\frac{{u}^{2}}{2}{|}_{2}^{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡2(16-4) \\\\  & =(6)\\operatorname{ln}⁡2\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364084 1075 #edb/ref 17592186364085 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364084 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364084 1077 "field-1" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364084 1079 #edb/ref 17592186364085 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364084 1080 #uuid "eb1fc348-9d4d-5007-8914-af996cd84800" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364085 1081 "6" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364085 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364085 1084 #uuid "eab9d45b-a0c0-5418-a203-0aba7763da28" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364086 1068 #edb/ref 17592186364087 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364086 1069 "q-351555" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364086 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364086 1071 "A particle moves along a straight line relative to a fixed origin $O$ with velocity $v(t)=5,$ where $t>0$ is the time. If the particle is at the position $x=-2$ when $t=3,$ calculate the position $x$ of the particle at time $t.$\n\n$x(t)=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364086 1072 #uuid "07e3331d-24dc-5a03-8255-432d659d4c85" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364086 1074 "We start by integrating the velocity to get $x(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =\\int v(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int 5\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =5t+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C$, we use the fact that $x(3)=-2.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2 & =5(3)+C \\\\ -2 & =15+C \\\\ C & =-17.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the position $x$ of the particle at time $t$ is \n\n$$\nx(t)=5t-17.\n$$" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364087 1075 #edb/ref 17592186364088 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364087 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364087 1077 "field-1" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364087 1079 #edb/ref 17592186364088 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364087 1080 #uuid "d883d07c-b8d1-51f8-94d3-219eb8fa8522" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364088 1081 "5t-17" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364088 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364088 1084 #uuid "a75c7b1e-d64f-5213-ae8b-449dc9777b72" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364089 1068 #edb/ref 17592186364090 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364089 1069 "q-351723" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364089 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364089 1071 "A particle moves along a straight line relative to a fixed origin $O$ with velocity $v(t)=\\frac{10}{(t+1)^{2}},$ where $t\\ge 0$ is the time. If the particle is at the position $x=4$ when $t=0,$ calculate the position $x$ of the particle at time $t.$\n\n$x(t)=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364089 1072 #uuid "9d5f3258-84b9-58c1-8e28-849f703c5897" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364089 1074 "We start by integrating the velocity to get $x(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =\\int v(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{10}{(t+1)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =10\\int (t+1)^{-2}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =10\\cdot \\frac{(t+1)^{-1}}{-1}+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{10}{t+1}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C$, we use the fact that $x(0)=4.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4 & =-\\frac{10}{0+1}+C \\\\ 4 & =-10+C \\\\ C & =14.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the position $x$ of the particle at time $t$ is \n\n$$\nx(t)=-\\frac{10}{t+1}+14.\n$$" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364090 1075 #edb/ref 17592186364091 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364090 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364090 1077 "field-1" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364090 1079 #edb/ref 17592186364091 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364090 1080 #uuid "60ab217d-abef-5106-a5eb-b9deda103f29" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364091 1081 "-\\frac{10}{t+1}+14" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364091 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364091 1084 #uuid "a45b6822-568d-5e04-a7d9-440011f9fae9" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364092 1068 #edb/ref 17592186364093 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364092 1069 "q-351769" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364092 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364092 1071 "A particle moves along a straight line relative to a fixed origin $O$ with acceleration \n\n$$\na(t)=\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{t+1}},\n$$\n\nwhere $t\\ge 0$ is the time. If the particle has position $x=4$ and velocity $v=-6$ when $t=0,$ the position $x$ of the particle at time $t$ is\n\n$x(t)=$\n{{field-1}}." 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364092 1072 #uuid "f6d76824-27f0-5491-801b-0afe50fb7ad3" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364092 1074 "We start by integrating the acceleration to get the velocity $v(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{t+1}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =3\\int (t+1)^{-1/2}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =3\\cdot \\frac{(t+1)^{1/2}}{1/2}+C \\\\  & =6\\sqrt{t+1}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C,$ we use the fact that $v(0)=-6.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-6 & =6\\sqrt{1}+C \\\\ -6 & =6+C \\\\ C & =-12.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the velocity is given by \n\n$$\nv(t)=6\\sqrt{t+1}-12.\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate the velocity to get $x(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =\\int v(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int (6\\sqrt{t+1}-12)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =6\\int (t+1)^{1/2}\\,\\text{d}t-12\\int \\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =6\\cdot \\frac{(t+1)^{3/2}}{3/2}-12t+K \\\\  & =4(t+1)^{3/2}-12t+K.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $K,$ we use the fact that $x(0)=4.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4 & =4(1)^{3/2}-0+K \\\\ 4 & =4+K \\\\ K & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the position $x$ of the particle at time $t$ is \n\n$$\nx(t)=4(t+1)^{3/2}-12t.\n$$" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364093 1075 #edb/ref 17592186364094 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364093 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364093 1077 "field-1" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364093 1079 #edb/ref 17592186364094 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364093 1080 #uuid "b7ba5aaf-330d-5532-9fbf-e11b36a85cdd" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364094 1081 "4(t+1)^{3/2}-12t" 1319413953