 expressed using sigma notation as" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364260 1072 #uuid "8fef8761-7152-5796-ba84-dc23d9280957" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364260 1074 "First, we must express the given series in the form\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{N}{a}_{1}{r}^{n-1},\n$$\n\nwhere ${a}_{1}$ denotes the first term of the series, $r$ denotes the common ratio, and $N$ denotes the number of terms in the series. In this case, ${a}_{1}=\\frac{1}{2}$ and $N=5.$\n\nTo find $r,$ we calculate the ratio of the first two terms in the series:\n\n$$\nr=\\frac{{a}_{2}}{{a}_{1}}=\\frac{(\\frac{1}{6})}{(\\frac{1}{2})}=\\frac{1}{3}\n$$\n\nHence, expressing the geometric series given in sigma notation, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{1}{6}+\\frac{1}{18}+\\frac{1}{54}+\\frac{1}{162}={\\sum }_{n=1}^{5}\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{3})}^{n-1}\\,\\,\n$$\n\nFinally, we can simplify the above series using the laws of exponents, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{5}\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{3})}^{n-1} & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{5}\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{3})}^{n}\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{3})}^{-1} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{5}\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{3})}^{-1}\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{3})}^{n} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{5}\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{3}{1}\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{3})}^{n} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{5}\\frac{3}{2}\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{3})}^{n}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364261 1075 #edb/ref 17592186364262 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364261 1075 #edb/ref 17592186364263 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364261 1075 #edb/ref 17592186364264 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364261 1075 #edb/ref 17592186364265 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364261 1075 #edb/ref 17592186364266 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364261 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364261 1077 "selection" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364