 shaded region above as a type I region in two-dimensional space?" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364341 1072 #uuid "b8dcd36b-62ff-51dd-911e-391155781896" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364341 1074 "A type I region can be written as\n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,a\\le x\\le b,\\,g(x)\\le y\\le f(x)},\n$$\n\nwhere $f$ and $g$ are continuous functions.\n\nTo express the given region as a type I region, we first rewrite all of the given curves as functions of $x:$\n\n$$\nx=y+1\\,⟹\\,y=x-1\n$$\n\nThe function $y=\\frac{1}{2}(x-1)^{2}$ is already written as a function of $x.$\n\nNotice that the region lies between the vertical lines $x=1$ and $x=3.$ So, we have $1\\le x\\le 3.$\n\nWe draw a vertical arrow through the region to determine the lower function $g(x)$ and the upper function $f(x).$ A vertical line should intersect the region in a single interval only for it to be a type I region.\n\n![](images/15/15f9c5d526747b80766a8a3250fa2e705d5d7d9561c52e08492b7a18f1483eb6.png)\n\nThe vertical line enters the region through the lower function $y=\\frac{1}{2}(x-1)^{2}$ and leaves through the upper function $y=x-1.$ So, we have $\\frac{1}{2}(x-1)^{2}\\le y\\le x-1.$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,1\\le x\\le 3,\\,\\frac{1}{2}(x-1)^{2}\\le y\\le x-1}.\n$$" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364342 1075 #edb/ref 17592186364343 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364342 1075 #edb/ref 17592186364344 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364342 1075 #edb/ref 17592186364345 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364342 1075 #edb/ref 17592186364346 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364342 1075 #edb/ref 17592186364347 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364342 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364342 1077 "selection" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364342 1079 #edb/ref 17592186364344 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364342 1080 #uuid "06791450-79a7-57ff-982c-0a9c0c48c94a" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364343 1081 "D={(x,y)\\,:\\,1\\le x\\le y+1,\\,0\\le y\\le \\frac{1}{2}(x-1)^{2}}" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364343 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364343 1084 #uuid "94d1678f-c40b-5af3-9c01-5e44121bc63a" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364344 1081 "D={(x,y)\\,:\\,1\\le x\\le 3,\\,\\frac{1}{2}(x-1)^{2}\\le y\\le x-1}" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364344 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364344 1084 #uuid "84793cb4-e8f5-5b93-983f-4e8cd175b741" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364345 1081 "D={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le 2,\\,0\\le x\\le \\sqrt{y}+1}" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364345 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364345 1084 #uuid "ef5ce8c7-a9c2-5498-ac15-36a28bbba91d" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364346 1081 "D={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le 2,\\,0\\le x\\le y+1}" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364346 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364346 1084 #uuid "a8532342-6134-55eb-82d5-4bcf4a8d4a3a" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364347 1081 "D={(x,y)\\,:\\,1\\le x\\le 3,\\,\\frac{1}{2}(x-1)^{2}\\le y\\le x+1}" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364347 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364347 1084 #uuid "c8beef18-d508-5038-9945-f9210d0f3975" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364348 1068 #edb/ref 17592186364349 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364348 1069 "q-72427" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364348 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364348 1071 "At which points on the curve ${y}^{2}+2x=2xy+2y-2$ is the tangent vector to the curve perpendicular to the vector $i?$" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364348 1072 #uuid "703e7046-bcfc-54c8-9bb1-c367d4d9ddb7" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364348 1074 "First, we bring all variables to one side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2}+2x & =2xy+2y-2 \\\\ {y}^{2}+2x-2xy-2y & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we set $f(x,y)={y}^{2}+2x-2xy-2y$, our curve can be viewed as the level curve $f(x,y)=-2.$  \n\nA tangent vector to the level curve can be computed as follows:\n\n$$\nt(x,y)={f}_{y}(x,y)\\,i-{f}_{x}(x,y)\\,j\n$$\n\nSo, we calculate $t(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t(x,y) & ={f}_{y}(x,y)\\,i-{f}_{x}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({y}^{2}+2x-2xy-2y)i-\\frac{∂}{∂x}({y}^{2}+2x-2xy-2y)j \\\\  & =(2y-2x-2)\\,i-(2-2y)\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince our tangent vector needs to be perpendicular to $i$, we must have $t\\cdot i=0.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t(x,y)\\cdot i & =0 \\\\ ((2y-2x-2)\\,i-(2-2y)\\,j)\\cdot i & =0 \\\\ 2y-2x-2 & =0 \\\\ 2(y-x-1) & =0 \\\\ y & =x+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $y=x+1$ into the equation of our curve gives:  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2}+2x-2xy-2y & =-2 \\\\ (x+1)^{2}+2x-2x(x+1)-2(x+1)+2 & =0 \\\\ (x+1)^{2}-2x(x+1)-2(x+1)+2(x+1) & =0 \\\\ (x+1)^{2}-2x(x+1)-\\cancel{2(x+1)}+\\cancel{2(x+1)} & =0 \\\\ (x+1)^{2}-2x(x+1) & =0 \\\\ (x+1)(x+1-2x) & =0 \\\\ (x+1)(-x+1) & =0 \\\\ x & =\\pm 1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally:\n\n- If $x=1$, we get $y=2$ and the corresponding point is $(1,2).$\n\n- If $x=-1$, we get $y=0$ and the corresponding point is $(-1,0).$" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364349 1075 #edb/ref 17592186364350 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364349 1075 #edb/ref 17592186364351 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364349 1075 #edb/ref 17592186364352 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364349 1075 #edb/ref 17592186364353 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364349 1075 #edb/ref 17592186364354 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364349 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592