edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364445 1071 "Given that ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} \\\\ {b}_{1}\\end{aligned}]$ and ${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{2} \\\\ {b}_{2}\\end{aligned}]$ are linearly independent eigenvectors of the matrix\n$A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 8 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}],$\nwhat is the value of $\\frac{{a}_{1}{a}_{2}}{{b}_{1}{b}_{2}}?$" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364445 1072 #uuid "0ee811a8-c053-59bb-b00f-f224606c0564" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364445 1074 "First, we need to find the eigenvalues of $A.$ To do that, we must solve the characteristic equation $det(A-\\lambda I)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}1-\\lambda  & 8 \\\\ -2 & 1-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (1-\\lambda )(1-\\lambda )-(-2)(8) & =0 \\\\ 1-2\\lambda +{\\lambda }^{2}+16 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-2\\lambda +17 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the quadratic formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lambda  & =\\frac{-b\\pm \\sqrt{{b}^{2}-4ac}}{2a} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\pm \\sqrt{(-2)^{2}-4(1)(17)}}{2(1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\pm \\sqrt{-64}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\pm 8\\text{i}}{2} \\\\  & =1\\pm 4\\text{i}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the eigenvalues are $\\lambda =1\\pm 4\\text{i}.$\n\nNow, let's consider the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=1+4\\text{i}.$ We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-{\\lambda }_{1}I & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 8 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}1+4\\text{i} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1+4\\text{i}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-4\\text{i} & 8 \\\\ -2 & -4\\text{i}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we get the system of linear equations\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-4\\text{i}{x}_{1}+8{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ -2{x}_{1}-4\\text{i}{x}_{2}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since the matrix $A-(1+4\\text{i})I$ is singular, the system above must have a non-zero solution. This means that one equation of the system is a multiple of another. As a result, we can drop one of the rows and take one of the variables as the free variable. For example, from the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n{x}_{2}=\\frac{1}{2}\\text{i}{x}_{1}\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}\\text{i}{x}_{1}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{1}\\in C.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{1}=2,$ we get the eigenvector\n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ \\text{i}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n \n\nThen, to compute the second eigenvector corresponding to ${\\lambda }_{2}=1-4\\text{i}$, we simply compute the complex conjugate of ${\\overset{―}{v}}_{1},$ and get\n\n$$\n{v}_{2}={\\overset{―}{v}}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -\\text{i}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{a}_{1}{a}_{2}}{{b}_{1}{b}_{2}} & =\\frac{(2)(2)}{(\\text{i})(-\\text{i})}=4.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364446 1075 #edb/ref 17592186364447 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364446 1075 #edb/ref 17592186364448 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364446 1075 #edb/ref 17592186364449 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364446 1075 #edb/ref 17592186364450 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364446 1075 #edb/ref 17592186364451 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364446 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364446 1077 "selection" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364446 1079 #edb/ref 17592186364451 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364446 1080 #uuid "16922444-299f-55a9-a2be-066c9885244c" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364447 1081 "-2\\text{i}" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364447 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364447 1084 #uuid "44822bc9-7e2e-562f-8058-69dd90f154f9" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364448 1081 "2\\text{i}" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364448 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533445 1759218604558