ight does not change. How fast is the surface area of the rectangular prism decreasing when its dimensions are $3\\,\\text{cm}$ in length and $10\\,\\text{cm}$ in width?" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364557 1072 #uuid "c144d86d-616b-51ad-b91f-c522e265b17b" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364557 1074 "Let $x,y,z$ be the dimensions of the solid, and let $V$ be its volume. We are given that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =3\\,\\text{cm}, \\\\ y & =10\\,\\text{cm}, \\\\ z & =5\\,\\text{cm}, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =-3\\,\\text{cm}\\text{/min}, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =5\\,\\text{cm}\\text{/min}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe would like to find $\\frac{\\text{d}S}{\\text{d}t}.$\n\nWe want to find the rate of change of $S$ knowing the rate of change of $x$ and $y.$ So, we start with the formula for the surface area of the rectangular prism:\n\n$$\nS=2xz+2xy+2yz\n$$\n\nSince $z=5\\,\\text{cm}$ is not changing, we get\n\n$$\nS=10x+2xy+10y.\n$$\n\nNow, we differentiate with respect to time using the chain rule, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}S}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(10x+2xy+10y) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}S}{\\text{d}t} & =(2x+10)(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})+(10+2y)(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now plug in the known values into the above expression. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}S}{\\text{d}t} & =(2\\cdot 3+10)(5)+(10+2\\cdot 10)(-3) \\\\  & =80-90 \\\\  & =-10\\,{\\text{cm}}^{2}\\text{/min}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface area of the rectangular prism is decreasing at a rate of $10\\,{\\text{cm}}^{2}/\\text{min}.$" 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364558 1075 #edb/ref 17592186364559 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364558 1075 #edb/ref 17592186364560 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364558 1075 #edb/ref 17592186364561 13194139533445 17592186045585 true] [17592186364558 1075 #edb/ref 17592